/* Track 9 — The Claude API (L2) */
(function(){
var L = window.LESSONS;

/* ------------------------------------------------ 9.1 */
L["9.1"] = {
  sub: "The API is the raw brain, programmable — where you stop USING Claude and start BUILDING things that use Claude for other people. Your first real request, demystified.",
  breath: "The Claude API takes your messages plus an API key and returns Claude's response as data your code controls — the same brain as the app, with no interface, priced per token, and it's the engine under every product in the money tracks.",
  asOf: true,
  hook: "<p>Everything you've built so far has an audience of one: you, driving. The API flips that. It's Claude with the interface stripped away — you send messages as data, you get a response as data, and YOUR code decides what happens next. That's the difference between a person using a tool and a business running on one. If Tracks 4–8 made you an operator, Track 9 makes you a builder — and it's where «make money with Claude» stops being services you deliver by hand and becomes products that serve people while you sleep.</p>",
  secs: [
    { h: "The mental model: brain-as-a-function",
      b: "<p>Strip away the chat window and Claude is a function: <strong>messages in → response out</strong>. The API is how your code calls that function. Three moving parts:</p><ul><li><strong>The endpoint</strong> — a web address you send a request to (the Messages API). Your code POSTs there; Claude's servers do the thinking; a response comes back.</li><li><strong>The API key</strong> — your credential and your meter. It authenticates you and bills you (per token — 1.3's prices). Get it from the Anthropic Console; treat it like a password (7.8's secrets law is now non-negotiable — a leaked key is someone spending YOUR money).</li><li><strong>The request/response</strong> — you send: which model, the messages, settings (max length, temperature…). You get back: the generated text, plus metadata (tokens used, why it stopped). Structured data your program reads, not prose you read.</li></ul><p>Everything else in this track is elaboration on that loop. Master the loop and the rest is vocabulary.</p>"},
    { h: "Your first request (you don't need to be a programmer)",
      b: "<p>Here's a complete Python request — and notice you can read it even if you don't «code», because you've been writing its parts for eight tracks:</p>" + PB("first_call.py — a complete API request", "import anthropic\n\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()  # reads ANTHROPIC_API_KEY from environment\n\nresponse = client.messages.create(\n    model=\"claude-sonnet-5\",              # the workhorse (1.3)\n    max_tokens=400,                        # output length cap\n    system=\"You are a concise travel assistant. Facts only; flag uncertainty.\",  # 2.6!\n    messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Three underrated day-trips from Pune, one line each.\"}\n    ],\n)\n\nprint(response.content[0].text)") + "<p>Read it with your trained eyes: <code>model</code> is 1.3's selection; <code>system</code> is 2.6's casting; <code>messages</code> is the conversation; <code>max_tokens</code> is 2.4's length control. <strong>The API isn't new concepts — it's the concepts you own, expressed as code.</strong> The SDK (that <code>anthropic</code> library) handles the web plumbing; you handle the intent — same division as every surface.</p>"},
    { h: "How to actually learn this (the Claude-Code way)",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>Build it WITH Claude Code</strong> (Track 7): «set up a Python project that calls the Claude API to do X» — it scaffolds, handles the key safely (.env, gitignored — 7.8), writes the call, runs it. You learn by commissioning and reading, exactly as 7.1 promised.</li><li><strong>The Console is your dashboard:</strong> get keys, watch spend, set spending limits (do this FIRST — 1.5's guardrails), and use the Workbench-style tooling to test prompts before coding them.</li><li><strong>Free-tier reality:</strong> the API is pay-per-token from the start (no free chatting allowance), but experiments cost pennies — a few hundred test calls on Haiku is a rounding error. Set a $5 limit and explore fearlessly.</li><li><strong>The bridge from here:</strong> once messages-in-response-out is muscle memory, the rest of this track adds senses (vision), hands (tools), guarantees (structured outputs), and economics (caching, batching) — each a well-defined upgrade to the same loop.</li></ul>"}
  ],
  worked: { t: "Worked example — the moment services become products",
    html: "<div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>Priya (our recurring freelancer) has a realisation.</em> She's been manually running her invoice-extraction skill (3.3→4.8) for clients — great money, but bounded by her hours. <em>The API changes the shape:</em> «what if the extraction ran automatically, for many clients, without me?» That sentence is the app→product transition, and it lives at the API.</p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>Her first API session (with Claude Code):</em> «set up a Python script that takes a folder of invoice images and calls the Claude API to extract them using this exact prompt [her proven contract], outputting a CSV. Handle the key via .env. Run it on these 5 test invoices.» Ten minutes later: her hand-crafted skill, now a program. The prompt is IDENTICAL — three tracks of prompt-craft transferred verbatim into the <code>messages</code> field.</p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The mindset shift she names out loud:</em> «In the app, I was the runtime. In the API, my code is the runtime, and I can point it at 10,000 invoices or wire it into a client's system.» Her contract-writing skill didn't change; its LEVERAGE did. <em>This is why the money tracks put products above services — and why they come AFTER this one. The API is the multiplier that turns skilled labour into a sellable machine.</em></p></div>"},
  lab: { where: "Claude Code + Anthropic Console",
    html: "<p>Make your first real API call — Claude Code does the heavy lifting. First: go to the Anthropic Console, create an API key, and SET A SPENDING LIMIT ($5 is plenty for learning). Then, in a fresh Claude Code project:</p>" + PB("prompt — first API build", "Set up a minimal Python project that calls the Claude API. Requirements: (1) read the API key from a .env file (create .env, add it to .gitignore — the key must NEVER be committed); (2) a script that sends a system prompt and a user message of my choosing and prints the response plus the token usage; (3) use the Haiku model so my experiments cost almost nothing. Then run it with this test: system='[YOUR CHOICE]', user='[YOUR CHOICE]'. After it works, explain each line of the API call in plain English, mapping it to concepts I already know (model selection, system prompts, message roles, max_tokens).", "prompt") + "<p>Then change the model to Sonnet and the same prompt — feel the quality/cost difference (1.3), now measured in real token counts.</p>",
    expect: "A working API call, a token count you can see, a key handled safely, and the click of recognition: the API is your existing skills in a new syntax. Total cost: pennies. You are now, technically, a Claude developer." },
  mistakes: [
    "<b>Skipping the spending limit.</b> The first thing to set, before the first call. A bug in a loop can spend real money fast — the Console limit is your seatbelt (1.5's guardrails, now mandatory).",
    "<b>Keys in code.</b> The single most expensive beginner mistake — a committed key is a public key is a drained account. .env + .gitignore, always (7.8, no exceptions).",
    "<b>«I need to learn to code first.»</b> You need to commission and read (7.1). The concepts are yours already; Claude Code writes the syntax.",
    "<b>Treating the API as a different, smarter Claude.</b> Same models (1.4's eternal law) — the API's power is programmability and scale, not intelligence.",
    "<b>Building before testing the prompt.</b> Test your prompt in the app or Console first (it's free-r and faster to iterate), THEN wire the proven version into code (3.6's eval-first, applied to shipping)."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "The fundamental thing the API gives you over the app:",
      opts: ["A smarter model", "Programmability — Claude becomes a function your code calls, so YOUR program (not you) is the runtime, enabling products and scale", "Free usage", "A nicer interface"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Same brain family (1.4).", "Correct — messages-in-response-out, called by code. Priya's realisation: services become products because the code runs the loop, not her hands.", "It's pay-per-token from call one — but experiments cost pennies.", "It has NO interface — that's the point; you build the interface."] },
    { q: "In the first_call.py example, the `system` parameter is:",
      opts: ["A new API-only concept", "2.6's system prompt / casting — standing instructions for the whole call — now a literal function argument", "The user's message", "The model name"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["You've written system prompts since Track 2 — same concept.", "Correct — the API isn't new ideas; it's your ideas as code. system=casting, messages=conversation, max_tokens=length control.", "The user message is in the messages array; system is separate.", "Model is the `model` argument."] },
    { q: "Before your first API call, the very first thing to do is:",
      opts: ["Write 500 lines of code", "Set a spending limit in the Console and set up .env/.gitignore for the key", "Cancel your subscription", "Memorise all model prices"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Claude Code writes the code; you set the guardrails.", "Correct — spending limit (seatbelt) + secret handling (7.8) are the two non-negotiables before any call. Pennies to learn, but only if a loop bug can't run away.", "The API is a separate meter (1.5) — subscription is unrelated.", "Know the tiers (1.3); memorising exact prices isn't the prerequisite."] }
  ],
  prac: [
    { tag: "warm", q: "Map every part of the first_call.py example to a lesson you've already learned. Which four concepts appear?",
      sol: "<b>model</b> → 1.3 model selection · <b>system</b> → 2.6 casting/system prompts · <b>messages</b> → the conversation (Track 2 throughout) · <b>max_tokens</b> → 2.4 output length control. Bonus: the SDK reading the key from environment → 7.8 secrets. The API is a syntax for skills you own." },
    { tag: "transfer", q: "Complete the lab, then modify it: add a second user message and an assistant message between them (a two-turn conversation) and observe how `messages` models a dialogue. What does this reveal about how the app works underneath?",
      sol: "<b>The reveal:</b> a conversation is just a list of {role, content} messages replayed on every call — the app is sending your WHOLE visible chat as this array each turn (1.2's context window, now visible as literal data). 'Memory within a chat' = the growing messages list. Seeing this makes the physics concrete: you're paying to re-send the history each turn, which is exactly why long chats cost more and why /compact exists." },
    { tag: "stretch", q: "Priya turned a manual skill into an API script. List the three things that are now TRUE that weren't before, and the one new responsibility she's taken on.",
      sol: "<b>Now true:</b> (1) it runs without her (scale + passive leverage); (2) it can be wired into other systems (a client's upload folder, a webhook); (3) its cost is now HER cost per run, not her subscription (the economics of Track 9.7 begin). <b>New responsibility:</b> she owns the reliability, security, and cost — a bug is her bug, a leaked key is her loss, a prompt that hallucinates ships to users without her eyes (1.6→3.4 must now be ENGINEERED into the code, not applied by hand). The API grants power and hands you the accountability that Cowork's UI used to hold. That trade is the whole builder phase." }
  ],
  recap: "The API is Claude as a function your code calls — messages in, response out, priced per token — expressing the very skills you already own (model choice, system prompts, format control) as code; set the spending limit and secure the key first, then build.",
  review: "From Lesson 1.4: the surface map said the API's superpower is «products and scale» — now you've made the first call that turns that phrase into a program.",
  bridge: "You can call Claude from code. Next: the full Messages API — multi-turn conversations, streaming, and the parameters that shape every response your product will ever generate.",
  deep: "<p><strong>Why «brain-as-a-function» is a profound shift, not just a convenience.</strong> The app makes Claude a DESTINATION (you go there, you interact). The API makes Claude a COMPONENT (it embeds invisibly inside other software). This is the same transition that turned electricity from «the thing at the power company» into an invisible utility inside every appliance — and it has the same consequence: value migrates from the utility to what's BUILT with it. In 2026, the «application layer» — products that use models via API to solve specific problems — is where most AI value and most AI businesses live, precisely because the raw model is a commodity available to everyone at list price (1.3), while a well-built product that applies it to a real problem is not. Your strategic position: you now hold both the operator skills (Tracks 4–8: using AI surfaces expertly) AND the builder entry point (this track: embedding AI in software). Few people hold both — most «AI users» never touch the API, and most «AI engineers» underrate the operator craft. That intersection is the whole thesis of the money tracks: you can DELIVER as an operator today and BUILD as a developer tomorrow, and the same prompt-craft powers both. The function you just called is the smallest possible business. The rest of this track makes it a real one.</p>"
};

/* ------------------------------------------------ 9.2 */
L["9.2"] = {
  sub: "The Messages API in full: conversations as data, the system parameter, streaming, and the dials (temperature, stop sequences, prefill) that shape every response.",
  breath: "Messages are a list of role-tagged turns you resend each call; the system parameter carries standing instructions; streaming delivers tokens live; and a handful of parameters (max_tokens, temperature, stop_sequences, prefill, thinking) tune behaviour precisely.",
  asOf: true,
  hook: "<p>You've made a call. Now learn the instrument it plays. The Messages API is small — a dozen concepts total — but each one is a dial your products will turn thousands of times, and setting them wrong is the difference between a chatbot that feels alive and one that feels broken. The good news: every dial maps to something you already understand from the chat window. This lesson is where «I called the API once» becomes «I can build with it.»</p>",
  secs: [
    { h: "Conversations are lists you resend",
      b: "<p>The core structure — and the fact that reframes everything:</p>" + PB("multi-turn.py — the conversation IS the array", "messages = [\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"I'm planning a budget trip to Goa.\"},\n    {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Great! What's your budget and how many days?\"},\n    {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"5 days, ₹15,000 total, I like beaches and food.\"},\n]\n# You resend this WHOLE list every call. Claude is stateless —\n# it has no memory of previous calls; the array IS the memory.", "prompt") + "<ul><li><strong>Stateless is the key word:</strong> the API remembers NOTHING between calls (1.2's «no memory between chats», now literal). Continuity is an illusion YOU maintain by resending the growing list. The app does this for you; your product must do it itself.</li><li><strong>Consequence 1 — cost grows with the conversation</strong> (you pay to reprocess the whole history each turn — 9.6's caching is the answer).</li><li><strong>Consequence 2 — YOU control the memory:</strong> trim old turns, summarise-and-replace (2.7's restart, in code), inject relevant context — memory management is a design decision your product makes, not a feature you're given.</li></ul>"},
    { h: "The system parameter and roles",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>system</strong> (separate from messages) — the standing instructions, casting, rules, truth-discipline (2.6, 3.4). This is where your product's SOUL lives; it's sent every call, above the conversation. The single most important string in any product you build.</li><li><strong>Roles</strong> — <code>user</code> and <code>assistant</code> alternate; the system is its own field. There's no «Claude» role invention — it's always these. Multi-turn = alternating user/assistant entries.</li><li><strong>Prefill — the API's secret dial</strong> (3.3, promised): end your messages with a partial <code>assistant</code> turn, and Claude continues FROM your words: start it with «{» to force JSON, with «VERDICT:» to skip preamble, with a chosen framing to constrain the answer. The first-tokens-are-rails principle (3.3), now mechanical.</li></ul>"},
    { h: "The behaviour dials",
      b: "<table><tr><th>Dial</th><th>What it does</th><th>Set it by</th></tr><tr><td><strong>max_tokens</strong></td><td>Hard cap on output length (required)</td><td>2.4's length control — set generously enough to finish, tight enough to bound cost</td></tr><tr><td><strong>temperature</strong></td><td>Randomness: 0 = deterministic/focused, higher = varied/creative</td><td>Low for extraction, classification, code (you want the same answer); higher for brainstorming, creative writing</td></tr><tr><td><strong>stop_sequences</strong></td><td>Strings that halt generation when produced</td><td>Cutting output at a delimiter — useful in structured pipelines</td></tr><tr><td><strong>thinking</strong></td><td>Extended thinking budget (2.5) on capable models</td><td>Enable + budget for hard reasoning; the flagship's adaptive thinking manages itself</td></tr><tr><td><strong>stream</strong></td><td>Deliver tokens as generated (the typing effect)</td><td>On for anything a human watches (felt latency); off for backend batch work</td></tr></table><p><strong>Streaming</strong> deserves a word: it changes NOTHING about cost or content and EVERYTHING about felt experience — a user watching words appear at 200ms tolerates what a 9-second silent spinner makes them abandon. Perceived performance is a product feature; streaming is how you buy it nearly free.</p>"}
  ],
  worked: { t: "Worked example — the same three dials making or breaking a product",
    html: "<div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The product:</em> a customer-support draft-reply generator. <em>Dial 1 — temperature:</em> the first build used the default and support replies came out subtly different each time for identical tickets — inconsistent, off-brand. Fix: temperature=0.3. Now the same ticket yields the same measured reply. <em>Extraction and support want CONSISTENCY; the dial was mis-set for the job.</em></p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>Dial 2 — system parameter:</em> the brand voice, the never-promise-refunds rule (2.2's edge law), the escalation triggers, the truth rules — all in <code>system</code>, sent every call. When the founder wanted a tone change, ONE string changed and every reply shifted. <em>The system prompt is the product's control panel — 2.6 was rehearsal for this.</em></p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>Dial 3 — streaming + prefill:</em> replies stream so the human agent watching feels instant response; and each reply is prefilled with «Draft reply:\\n» so there's zero preamble — it starts with the usable text. <em>Three dials, correctly set, turned a wonky demo into a shippable tool.</em> Notice none of it was new knowledge — temperature is «consistency vs variety», system is casting, prefill is first-tokens-are-rails. The API is your judgment, parameterised.</p></div>"},
  lab: { where: "Claude Code",
    html: "<p>Build a two-turn conversation script and play the dials. Commission this:</p>" + PB("prompt — dials playground", "Build a Python script that: (1) maintains a messages list across a 3-turn conversation (I'll type each user turn, it appends the assistant reply and continues); (2) uses a system prompt I define; (3) exposes temperature and max_tokens as easy-to-change variables at the top; (4) streams the output so I see tokens appear live. Then run it twice with the SAME conversation: once at temperature=0, once at temperature=1.0, and show me both full transcripts so I can compare. Explain what changed and why.", "prompt") + "<p>Then add a prefill: make the assistant's replies always start with a fixed phrase, and observe how it constrains the output.</p>",
    expect: "A live-streaming multi-turn conversation, a visible temperature comparison (0 = stable, 1.0 = varied), and prefill steering the opening. You've now operated every core Messages dial — which is most of what building on the API requires." },
  mistakes: [
    "<b>Forgetting statelessness.</b> Expecting the API to «remember» across calls — it doesn't; you resend the array. Products that forget this ship amnesiac bots.",
    "<b>Default temperature for consistency tasks.</b> Extraction/classification/support want low temperature — the default's variety is a bug when you need repeatability.",
    "<b>High temperature for creativity, believing it's «smarter».</b> It's more random, not more capable — creativity comes from the prompt (Track 2); temperature just widens the spread.",
    "<b>Thin system prompts.</b> The system parameter is your product's soul and control panel — under-investing here is under-building the product (2.6→3.4 all belong in it).",
    "<b>No streaming on human-facing output.</b> Free perceived-performance left on the table; silent spinners cause abandonment that streaming would've prevented."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "«The API is stateless» means:",
      opts: ["It's unreliable", "It remembers nothing between calls — you resend the full messages list each time, so YOU own memory management", "It can't hold a conversation", "It has no system prompt"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Stateless ≠ unreliable — it's a precise property.", "Correct — continuity is your product's job (resend, trim, summarise). The app hides this; your code must do it. This is 1.2's physics as an API fact.", "It holds conversations beautifully — via the array you maintain.", "system is a core parameter; statelessness is about cross-call memory."] },
    { q: "For a classification pipeline that must give the same answer to the same input, set temperature:",
      opts: ["High — for creativity", "Low (near 0) — you want deterministic, repeatable output", "It doesn't matter", "Exactly 0.5 always"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Creativity is the enemy of a classifier — you want boring consistency.", "Correct — low temperature for extraction/classification/code; high for brainstorming/creative. The dial matches the job (Priya's support-bot fix).", "It matters enormously for consistency-critical tasks.", "0.5 is a middle nobody specifically needs; match the dial to the task."] },
    { q: "Streaming changes:",
      opts: ["The cost and the content", "Neither cost nor content — only FELT latency (tokens appear live), which is a real product feature for anything a human watches", "The model's intelligence", "The token count"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Identical tokens, identical price.", "Correct — perceived performance, bought nearly free. A watched stream beats a silent spinner for abandonment, always.", "Same brain.", "Same tokens — just delivered incrementally."] }
  ],
  prac: [
    { tag: "warm", q: "For each task, pick a temperature and say why: (a) extracting dates from documents, (b) generating 20 brand-name ideas, (c) writing SQL from a question, (d) drafting a poem.",
      sol: "(a) ~0 — one right extraction (b) high (~1) — want variety/spread (c) ~0 — code must be correct and consistent (d) high — creative variety. The pattern: correctness/consistency → low; exploration/creativity → high. Temperature is a spread dial, not a smart dial." },
    { tag: "transfer", q: "Complete the lab, then implement a simple memory-management strategy: when the conversation exceeds N turns, summarise the oldest turns into one system-appended note and drop them. What real product problem does this solve?",
      sol: "<b>Solves:</b> unbounded cost and context-window overflow (1.2/1.3) in long-running conversations — a chatbot that runs for hours can't resend an ever-growing array forever. Summarise-and-drop (2.7's restart, coded) keeps cost bounded and the desk clean. Every production chatbot implements some version of this; you just built the core of it." },
    { tag: "stretch", q: "Your product's system prompt is where its behaviour AND its security live. Given prompt injection (8.5), what belongs in the system prompt to defend it, and what's the honest limit of system-prompt defense?",
      sol: "<b>Belongs there:</b> role and rules, explicit instruction to treat user/tool content as data not commands, refusal boundaries, output constraints — all reinforcing the instruction hierarchy (8.5's model layer). <b>Honest limit:</b> the system prompt is ONE layer — a determined injection can still attempt to override it, because it's all one token stream (8.5's core truth). So system-prompt hardening is necessary but never sufficient: it pairs with structural defenses (break the trifecta, least-privilege tools, human hinges on consequential actions, sandboxing). Building the system prompt IS partly security engineering — which is exactly why 8.5 came before this track. You defend in depth; the system string is the innermost, not the only, wall." }
  ],
  recap: "Conversations are stateless arrays you resend and manage; the system parameter is your product's soul; and the dials — temperature for spread, prefill for rails, streaming for felt speed, max_tokens for bounds — are your judgment made mechanical.",
  review: "From Lesson 2.5: extended thinking was a chat toggle; here it's a `thinking` parameter with a budget — the same deliberation dial, now programmable per request.",
  bridge: "Text mastered. Next: senses — sending images and PDFs to the API, so your products can read receipts, analyse charts, and process documents at scale.",
  deep: "<p><strong>Statelessness is a feature, not a limitation — and understanding why unlocks scalable design.</strong> A stateless API means each call is independent: no server-side session to maintain, no memory to synchronise, infinite horizontal scale (any server can handle any request because none holds state). This is the same architectural choice that let the web scale (HTTP is stateless; your login «session» is a cookie you resend — exactly like resending the messages array). The design freedom it grants you: memory becomes a CHOICE with options — resend everything (simple, costly), summarise (bounded, lossy), retrieve relevant history from a database (RAG over conversation — sophisticated), or hybrid. Production systems in 2026 mix these: recent turns verbatim, older turns summarised, relevant facts retrieved from a vector store, all assembled into each call's array. You'll meet the machinery (Managed Agents' memory stores — 10.4) that automates this, but the PRINCIPLE is what you learned here: the model is a stateless function; intelligence about what to feed it is the application's job, and that job is where much of the engineering value (and the differentiation between products) actually lives. The prompt is the model's input; the CONTEXT ASSEMBLY that produces that input is the product.</p>"
};

/* ------------------------------------------------ 9.3 */
L["9.3"] = {
  sub: "Vision and documents on the API: sending images and PDFs so your products can read receipts, analyse charts, and process paperwork at scale.",
  breath: "The API accepts images (via base64, URL, or the Files API) and PDFs (text + visual understanding, hundreds of pages), so the reading skills you used in chat (4.4) become programmable pipelines — with the same eyes-for-judgment, code-for-math discipline.",
  asOf: true,
  hook: "<p>A restaurant chain has 40,000 supplier invoices a year as photos. A logistics firm has a decade of shipping documents as scanned PDFs. An insurer processes claims that are mostly pictures of damage. None of this is text — and all of it is exactly the work that used to require armies of data-entry staff. The API's vision turns «a human looks at each one» into «a program reads all of them» — and you already know the discipline from 4.4; this lesson makes it programmable.</p>",
  secs: [
    { h: "Sending images",
      b: "<p>Three ways to give Claude an image, same result:</p>" + PB("vision.py — an image in a message", "message = client.messages.create(\n    model=\"claude-sonnet-5\",\n    max_tokens=500,\n    messages=[{\n        \"role\": \"user\",\n        \"content\": [\n            {\"type\": \"image\", \"source\": {  # base64, url, or file_id\n                \"type\": \"base64\", \"media_type\": \"image/jpeg\",\n                \"data\": image_base64,\n            }},\n            {\"type\": \"text\", \"text\": \"Extract vendor, date, total, and line items as JSON. Unreadable fields = null, never guess.\"},\n        ],\n    }],\n)", "prompt") + "<ul><li><strong>Content becomes a list</strong> when mixing images and text — images and instructions in one message. Multiple images per message are supported (comparisons, multi-page).</li><li><strong>Three sources:</strong> base64 (embed the bytes), URL (point to a hosted image), or Files API (upload once, reference by ID — best for reuse; below).</li><li><strong>It's real vision</strong> (4.4): charts, handwriting, diagrams, screenshots, damage photos. And the extraction contract (3.3) rides in the text part — «null never guess» is doing the same job it always did.</li></ul>"},
    { h: "PDFs: documents at document scale",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>Native PDF support:</strong> send a PDF and Claude reads BOTH its text and its visual layout — tables, charts, form fields, scanned pages (it sees page images too). Hundreds of pages per request (the exact ceiling scales with model/context — 1M-context models go furthest; verify current limits in docs).</li><li><strong>This is the 3.2 long-context skill, applied to real documents:</strong> the quotes-first grounding, the ABSENT-not-invented rule, the docs-top-task-bottom layout — all transfer. A 200-page contract analysis in chat is a 200-page contract analysis in a pipeline.</li><li><strong>The killer pattern:</strong> structured extraction from document floods — invoices, claims, contracts, forms, statements → clean data, at volume, with the totality and null-honesty contracts (3.3) enforcing trustworthiness. This is a HUGE slice of the real-world money in AI, because every business drowns in documents (6.2's spreadsheet-epidemic point, generalised to all paperwork).</li></ul>"},
    { h: "The Files API and the vision discipline",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>Files API:</strong> upload a file once, get an ID, reference it across many calls — instead of re-sending a big PDF's bytes every time. Saves bandwidth and, with caching (9.6), money. The right way to handle documents you'll query repeatedly.</li><li><strong>The discipline is 4.4's, non-negotiable at scale:</strong> eyes for reading/judgment, CODE for the math. If your pipeline needs «sum of all invoice totals», Claude extracts each total (vision) and your CODE sums them (arithmetic) — never ask the model to total 40,000 numbers in its head (4.4's professor/calculator law, at industrial scale).</li><li><strong>Verification scales by form</strong> (5.3): you can't eyeball 40,000 extractions — so build reconciliation (totals that should match a control figure), sampling (spot-check N random extractions against sources), and confidence/UNREADABLE flags routing the doubtful to humans. The 3.4 truth stack, in production.</li><li><strong>Cost awareness:</strong> images and PDFs consume tokens (vision has real token cost; big PDFs are big) — model choice matters (Haiku for simple extraction volume, Sonnet/flagship for nuanced documents — 1.3), and 9.6/9.7's economics become load-bearing.</li></ul>"}
  ],
  worked: { t: "Worked example — the invoice pipeline, from skill to system",
    html: "<div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>Priya's invoice extraction (9.1) meets vision.</em> Her clients photograph supplier invoices; she builds the pipeline: each image → API call with her extraction contract in the text part → structured row out. <em>The contract is UNCHANGED from 3.3</em> — schema by example, null-honesty, status field. Vision just changed the input from typed text to a photo.</p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>Scaling it right:</em> she processes on Haiku (simple extraction × high volume — 1.3's descend-for-volume), uploads recurring supplier templates via the Files API, and — critically — builds the verification IN: her code sums the extracted totals and reconciles against each client's control figure; mismatches and UNREADABLE-flagged invoices route to a human review queue (5.3's receipts + 3.4's routing, coded). <em>The pipeline's job isn't to be perfect; it's to be trustworthy and to know what it doesn't know.</em></p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The business shape:</em> what was «Priya extracts invoices for ₹X/hour» is now «Priya's system extracts invoices at ₹Y per thousand, running while she sleeps, with a human-review queue she checks daily.» Same skill, transformed leverage — and the reconciliation + sampling are what let her PROMISE accuracy to clients (which is what they actually pay for). <em>Vision didn't just add a feature; it turned bounded labour into a scalable, trustworthy service — the exact app→product leap the money tracks monetise.</em></p></div>"},
  lab: { where: "Claude Code",
    html: "<p>Build a real vision extraction pipeline on your own documents (receipts, invoices, forms, ID cards — anything with structured data in image/PDF form):</p>" + PB("prompt — vision pipeline", "Build a Python pipeline that: (1) takes a folder of document images (or a PDF); (2) for each, calls the Claude API (Haiku for cost) with an extraction prompt I'll provide — my schema is [YOUR FIELDS], and the rule is: unreadable fields = null, never guessed; each result includes a status (OK/UNREADABLE); (3) writes all results to a CSV with a source_file column; (4) at the end, prints receipts: count in vs out, and lists any UNREADABLE items for my review. Run it on my [N] test documents. Then I'll spot-check 3 against their sources.", "prompt") + "<p>Verify: does the count reconcile? Do the 3 spot-checks match? Did unreadable docs get flagged rather than guessed?</p>",
    expect: "A working document-to-data pipeline with built-in receipts and honest UNREADABLE handling — the exact shape of a sellable service. Notice the extraction PROMPT is pure Track 3; vision and the API are just the delivery mechanism. You've built industrial data entry that never lies silently." },
  mistakes: [
    "<b>Asking the model to do the aggregate math.</b> Extract per-document (vision), sum in CODE (4.4's law at scale) — never «total these 5,000 invoices» in one prompt.",
    "<b>No reconciliation or sampling.</b> 40,000 extractions you can't eyeball demand machine-checkable receipts (control totals) + human sampling (5.3). Trust at scale is engineered, not hoped.",
    "<b>Dropping the null-honesty contract.</b> A vision pipeline that guesses unreadable fields ships confident wrong data into business systems — 3.3's null rule is MORE important at scale, not less.",
    "<b>Flagship model for simple high-volume extraction.</b> Haiku reads receipts fine; paying 12× for volume extraction is the classic model mis-selection (1.3) that kills product margins (9.7).",
    "<b>Re-sending big PDFs every call.</b> Files API (upload once, reference by ID) exists precisely for repeated document access — bandwidth and cost saved."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "For «sum the totals across 10,000 extracted invoices», the correct design is:",
      opts: ["Ask Claude to add them all in one prompt", "Claude extracts each total (vision); your CODE sums them (arithmetic) — model for reading, code for math", "Use a bigger model to do the math", "Round and estimate"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Models don't reliably total thousands of numbers — that's hallucination-at-scale (4.4).", "Correct — the professor/calculator division, industrial edition. Vision reads; code computes; the sum is a real fact.", "Bigger models still shouldn't do bulk arithmetic — the whole point is delegating math to deterministic code.", "Estimation in a financial pipeline is malpractice."] },
    { q: "The extraction CONTRACT (schema, null-honesty, status) in a vision pipeline is:",
      opts: ["A new vision-specific requirement", "The exact same 3.3 contract you wrote for text — vision only changed the input format from text to image/PDF", "Optional at scale", "Handled automatically"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["It's your existing contract, unchanged.", "Correct — the skill transferred verbatim; the input got a new form. This is the course's recurring payoff: principles written small run large.", "MORE critical at scale — you can't catch silent guesses across 40,000 docs by eye.", "You must write it — the API extracts what your prompt demands."] },
    { q: "Why does document extraction represent a huge slice of real AI money?",
      opts: ["Documents are rare", "Every business drowns in paperwork (invoices, claims, forms, contracts) that used to require manual data entry — turning that flood into clean data at volume is directly valuable", "It's technically impressive", "It's not — it's a toy use case"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["The opposite — paperwork is universal and endless.", "Correct — the document-to-data pipeline attacks a cost center every business has. Priya's leap (bounded labour → scalable service) is the template, and it's eminently sellable (Track 11).", "Impressiveness isn't the value; solving the paperwork tax is.", "It's one of the most reliably profitable applied AI patterns in 2026."] }
  ],
  prac: [
    { tag: "warm", q: "Which needs vision, and which of those also needs a code layer for math? (a) reading a scanned contract's termination clause, (b) totalling expenses from 500 receipt photos, (c) describing what's in a product photo, (d) checking if 200 forms are all signed.",
      sol: "(a) vision only — reading/judgment (b) <b>vision + code</b> — extract each amount (vision), sum (code) (c) vision only (d) <b>vision + code</b> — detect signature presence per form (vision), count/report the unsigned (code). The vision-then-code pattern is most real document jobs." },
    { tag: "transfer", q: "Build the lab pipeline on real documents, then add the trust layer: reconciliation against a control total AND a random 5-sample audit. Report whether your pipeline is production-trustworthy and how you know.",
      sol: "<b>Production-trustworthy means:</b> counts reconcile (totality), a control figure matches (accuracy at aggregate), sampled extractions are correct (accuracy at instance), and unreadables are flagged not guessed (honesty). If all four hold on your test set, you can PROMISE a client accuracy — which is the difference between a demo and a service (and what justifies the price in 11.4)." },
    { tag: "stretch", q: "Design the full architecture for a document-processing service handling 50,000 mixed documents/month for a client, including model selection, cost control, verification, and the human-in-the-loop. What's the pitch to the client?",
      sol: "<b>Architecture:</b> Haiku for simple/high-volume docs, Sonnet for complex/nuanced ones (routing by type — a cheap classifier first); Files API + caching (9.6) for recurring templates; extraction contracts with null-honesty; reconciliation + sampling for verification; a human review queue for UNREADABLE/low-confidence/reconciliation-mismatch items only (concentrating human attention on the ~5% that needs it — 3.4's routing principle). Batch API (9.6) for the non-urgent bulk at 50% off. <b>The pitch:</b> «I turn your 50k/month document pile into clean data at ₹X, with a verified accuracy guarantee and full audit trail — your staff review only the exceptions, not everything.» Notice the pitch sells VERIFICATION and TRUST, not extraction — the model does extraction; YOU engineer the trust, and trust is the product (Track 11's whole thesis, previewed in code)." }
  ],
  recap: "Vision and PDFs make chat's reading skills programmable at scale — same extraction contracts, same eyes-for-judgment/code-for-math law, plus reconciliation and sampling to make bulk extraction trustworthy; the document-to-data pipeline is a huge slice of real AI money.",
  review: "From Lesson 4.4: «eyes for reading, code for counting» was a chat habit — at API scale it becomes the architecture of every trustworthy document pipeline you'll build or sell.",
  bridge: "Claude can read your documents. Next, the leap that makes products truly capable: tool use — giving Claude hands to call functions, query databases, and act in the world.",
  deep: "<p><strong>Multimodal extraction and the end of manual data entry.</strong> «Data entry» employed millions for decades because documents (invoices, forms, records) were designed for human eyes, not databases — bridging that gap meant human labour. Optical character recognition (OCR) automated the easy 80% (clean printed text) but broke on the hard 20% that mattered: varied layouts, handwriting, context («which number is the total?»), tables, mixed languages. Vision-capable LLMs close the gap differently — not by reading characters but by UNDERSTANDING documents the way a person does (this IS the total because it's labelled and it's the largest figure and it's at the bottom). The 2026 consequence is a genuine labour-market shift: routine document processing is rapidly automating, and the human role migrates to EXCEPTION HANDLING and VERIFICATION (your review queue) — fewer people, doing higher-judgment work. For your career this cuts two ways, and honesty demands both: it's a headwind for pure data-entry roles, and a tailwind for anyone who can BUILD and OPERATE the pipelines that replace them (you). The document flood isn't shrinking; who processes it is changing — and the processors are now people who write extraction contracts and engineer verification, which is precisely what this lesson trained. Every «boring» document pipeline you can build is a cost center you can attack for a client, priced against the salaries it replaces (11.1's economics). Unglamorous, universal, and directly monetizable — the best kind of skill.</p>"
};

/* ------------------------------------------------ 9.4 */
L["9.4"] = {
  sub: "Tool use: giving Claude hands. The mechanism that lets your products query databases, call APIs, run calculations, and act in the world — the foundation of every agent.",
  breath: "Tool use lets you define functions (name, description, input schema) that Claude can call — it emits a structured request, YOUR code runs the function and returns the result, and Claude continues with real data or a completed action; it's the same loop as MCP (8.1), now in your own code.",
  asOf: true,
  hook: "<p>Until now, your API products could only TALK — generate text from what they were given. Tool use lets them ACT: look up a real order, check live inventory, do exact math, send a message, book a slot. It's the single most important API capability for building anything beyond a chatbot, and — beautiful news — you already understand it completely, because it's the MCP loop (8.1) with you writing both sides. This lesson makes «AI that does things» something you can build.</p>",
  secs: [
    { h: "The loop you already know",
      b: "<p>You saw this exact loop in 8.1 (MCP). Now YOU own both ends:</p><ul><li><strong>1. You define tools</strong> — each with a name, a description (the prompt by which Claude decides when to use it — 8.1's law), and an input schema (typed parameters). You send these with your request.</li><li><strong>2. Claude decides and requests</strong> — reading the conversation, if a tool fits, Claude returns <code>stop_reason: 'tool_use'</code> and a structured call: which tool, what arguments. <strong>It does NOT run anything</strong> (8.1's crucial division — the model emits; your code executes).</li><li><strong>3. YOUR code executes</strong> — you run the actual function (query the database, call the weather API, compute), get a result.</li><li><strong>4. You return the result</strong> — append it as a <code>tool_result</code> and call again; Claude continues with real data, either answering or calling another tool.</li></ul>" + PB("tool_use.py — defining a tool", "tools = [{\n    \"name\": \"get_order_status\",\n    \"description\": \"Look up a customer order's current status by order ID. \"\n                   \"Use when the user asks about the state, shipping, or \"\n                   \"delivery of a specific order. Not for creating or \"\n                   \"cancelling orders.\",\n    \"input_schema\": {\n        \"type\": \"object\",\n        \"properties\": {\"order_id\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n        \"required\": [\"order_id\"],\n    },\n}]\n# Send tools=tools with your request. When Claude returns a tool_use\n# block, run get_order_status(order_id) yourself and return the result.", "prompt") + "<p>Recognise it? The description follows every 2.2/8.4 rule (what, when, when-NOT); the schema is 3.3's typed contract. Tool design IS the interface-craft you learned building MCP servers — same skill, your own code.</p>"},
    { h: "Client tools vs server tools",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>Client tools (you define, you run):</strong> the pattern above — YOUR functions, executed by YOUR code. This is how products connect Claude to their own databases, business logic, and external APIs. Total control, total responsibility.</li><li><strong>Server tools (Anthropic-provided, they run):</strong> built-in tools Claude can use that Anthropic executes — <strong>web search</strong> (live facts, 1.6's defense as an API feature), <strong>code execution</strong> (Claude writes and runs code in a sandbox — 4.4's analysis tool, programmable), <strong>computer use</strong> (screen control — 6.1's engine). You enable them; you don't implement them. Best of both: capability without building it.</li><li><strong>Structured mode & parallel calls:</strong> strict schemas guarantee valid tool inputs; Claude can request MULTIPLE tools in one turn (check inventory AND price simultaneously) — your code runs them and returns all results. Efficiency and power.</li></ul>"},
    { h: "Tool use IS the agent foundation",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>An agent is a tool-use loop that keeps going</strong> (Track 10's whole subject, previewed): Claude calls a tool → sees the result → decides the next tool → repeats until the goal is met. Everything agentic — Cowork, Claude Code, Managed Agents — is this loop, elaborated. Understanding tool use is understanding agents at the mechanism level.</li><li><strong>The security weight lands here</strong> (8.5, now yours to hold): tools are the ACTION CHANNEL — the third leg of the lethal trifecta. A tool that sends email, deletes records, or spends money is exactly where injection becomes dangerous. Design tools with least privilege (read vs write vs destructive — 8.2), human hinges on consequential ones (4.7), and the trifecta audit on any agent you build (8.5). Tool DESIGN is security design.</li><li><strong>Errors that teach</strong> (8.4): a tool returning «order_id not found; did you mean one of: …» lets Claude self-correct — your tools' error messages are part of the conversation. Design them as such.</li></ul>"}
  ],
  worked: { t: "Worked example — the support bot that actually helps",
    html: "<div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The upgrade:</em> Priya's support-draft bot (9.2) could only write plausible replies — it couldn't KNOW anything. Tool use fixes that. She defines three client tools: get_order_status (read), get_return_policy (read), create_ticket (write — for escalation). <em>Now the bot can look things up before it speaks.</em></p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The loop in action:</em> customer asks «where's my order #4471?» → Claude emits get_order_status(order_id='4471') → Priya's code queries the real database → returns «shipped, arriving Tuesday» → Claude drafts an accurate, specific reply. <em>The bot went from confidently-vague to actually-correct</em> — because tools replaced hallucination-prone recall with real lookups (1.6's grounding, mechanised).</p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The security design (8.5 in practice):</em> the two read tools run freely; create_ticket (the write/action tool — trifecta leg 3) is behind a confirmation and scoped so it can ONLY create tickets, never modify orders or access other customers' data (least privilege — 8.2). She runs the trifecta audit: the bot reads untrusted customer messages (leg 2) and has database access (leg 1) — so the action tool's constraint (create-ticket-only, confirmed) is what keeps a hostile message from becoming a breach. <em>Tool use made the bot capable AND made security a design problem she solved deliberately — which is exactly the builder's job.</em></p></div>"},
  lab: { where: "Claude Code",
    html: "<p>Build a tool-using assistant for something real — a lookup over your own data (a CSV, a small database, a JSON file). Commission:</p>" + PB("prompt — tool-use build", "Build a Python assistant that uses Claude tool use over my data in [FILE]. Define these tools: [1-2 READ tools — e.g., look_up_record(id), search_records(query)]. Implement the full loop: send tools, detect tool_use responses, run the actual function against my data, return tool_result, continue until Claude answers. Then let me have a conversation with it. Rules: the tools are READ-ONLY (no writes/deletes); each tool's description follows what/when/when-not; errors return a helpful message Claude can act on (e.g., 'not found; closest matches: ...'). Show me the tool_use requests Claude emits so I can watch the loop.", "prompt") + "<p>Watch the loop: your question → Claude's tool call → your data → Claude's grounded answer. Try a query with no match and see the error-message-teaches pattern work.</p>",
    expect: "An assistant that ANSWERS FROM YOUR REAL DATA instead of guessing — the difference between a chatbot and a useful product. Seeing Claude emit a structured tool call and then use your returned data closes the loop that 8.1 opened. You've built the mechanism under every agent." },
  mistakes: [
    "<b>Thinking Claude runs the tool.</b> Claude REQUESTS; your code executes (8.1's division). Products that misunderstand this can't be built correctly.",
    "<b>Vague tool descriptions.</b> Claude picks tools by description (8.1/8.4) — «gets data» causes mis-calls; «looks up order status by ID; use for shipping/delivery questions; not for cancellations» steers correctly.",
    "<b>Action tools without least privilege or hinges.</b> The write/destructive tool is the trifecta's action leg (8.5) — scope it tightly, confirm consequential ones. Tool design is security design.",
    "<b>Errors that just fail.</b> Tool errors are read by Claude — «not found; did you mean X?» self-heals; «Error 500» wastes the turn (8.4).",
    "<b>Building custom tools for what server tools provide.</b> Need web facts? Enable web search. Need computation? Code execution. Don't rebuild what Anthropic runs for you."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "In the tool-use loop, when Claude returns a tool_use block, it has:",
      opts: ["Run the tool and gotten the answer", "REQUESTED a tool call (which tool, what arguments) — YOUR code must execute it and return the result for Claude to continue", "Failed", "Ended the conversation"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Never — the model emits the request; it has no hands (8.1's division, now in your code).", "Correct — emit → you execute → you return → Claude continues. This is THE loop, and owning both ends is how products act.", "It's the loop working as designed.", "It's a pause for your code to act, then continue."] },
    { q: "Why is tool use where the security weight lands?",
      opts: ["Tools are slow", "Tools are the ACTION CHANNEL — the third leg of the lethal trifecta — so a product that reads untrusted input and has consequential tools is exactly the dangerous shape (8.5)", "Tools can't be secured", "It doesn't"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Speed is irrelevant.", "Correct — tool design IS security design: least privilege, human hinges on consequential tools, trifecta audit. Priya's create-ticket-only constraint is this in practice.", "They're secured precisely through design (scoping, hinges) — that's the builder's job.", "It's the single most security-relevant API capability."] },
    { q: "An agent, at the mechanism level, is:",
      opts: ["A different, smarter model", "A tool-use loop that keeps going — call a tool, see the result, decide the next, repeat until the goal is met", "A chatbot with a personality", "Impossible to build yourself"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Same models; the LOOP is what makes it an agent.", "Correct — Cowork, Claude Code, Managed Agents are all this loop elaborated. Track 10 builds it; you now understand its atom.", "Agents can have personas, but the definition is the autonomous tool loop.", "You just built the mechanism in the lab — agents are this, iterated (10.1)."] }
  ],
  prac: [
    { tag: "warm", q: "Write the description (what/when/when-not) for a tool `check_availability(date, service)` in a booking system, and classify it: read, write, or destructive?",
      sol: "<b>Description:</b> «Check open appointment slots for a given date and service. Use when the user asks about availability, timings, or whether they can book. Not for making a booking (use book_appointment) or cancelling.» <b>Classification: READ</b> — safe to run freely, no confirmation needed. Its companion book_appointment would be WRITE (confirm + scope), and there'd deliberately be NO delete_all_bookings (8.4's absent-beats-guarded)." },
    { tag: "transfer", q: "Complete the lab, then add ONE write tool (e.g., save a note, log a request) — but design its security: least privilege, a confirmation hinge, and run the trifecta audit on the whole assistant. Report the audit.",
      sol: "<b>Trifecta audit shape:</b> does it read untrusted input (your queries — trusted, so leg 2 may be absent, making it safe)? does it access private data (your file — leg 1)? does the write tool create an action channel (leg 3)? If it's YOUR data and YOUR queries, legs 1+3 without untrusted content = manageable. But note: the moment this assistant reads UNTRUSTED input (customer messages, web content), all three legs assemble and the write tool needs the hinge. You've now designed tool security from first principles — the exact skill 8.5 demanded and Track 10 requires." },
    { tag: "stretch", q: "Compare: when should a builder use their own client tools vs Anthropic's server tools vs an MCP server (Track 8)? Give the decision rule and an example of each.",
      sol: "<b>Client tools:</b> for YOUR specific business logic and data — e.g., get_order_status over your database (nobody else has your schema). <b>Server tools:</b> for common capabilities Anthropic runs — web search (live facts), code execution (math/analysis), computer use — don't rebuild these. <b>MCP server:</b> when the tool/integration should be REUSABLE across products and clients, or shared/sold, or already exists as a standard server (8.3) — e.g., a Slack or database MCP server you or the vendor built once and any AI can use. <b>Decision rule:</b> unique-to-you-and-code-only → client tool; common-capability-Anthropic-provides → server tool; reusable/shareable/standard → MCP. The three compose in real products: a support agent might use client tools (your DB) + a server tool (web search for shipping carrier status) + an MCP server (your CRM). Knowing which is which is builder-grade architecture — and 'design our AI's tool layer' is a real 2026 engagement (11.2, dev tier)." }
  ],
  recap: "Tool use gives Claude hands: you define tools (name, steering description, typed schema), Claude requests calls, your code executes and returns — client tools for your logic, server tools for Anthropic's capabilities, and tool design IS security design because tools are the trifecta's action leg.",
  review: "From Lesson 8.1: the MCP tool-call loop you learned to READ, you now WRITE — both ends, in your own product. And from 8.5: the action channel is where the trifecta bites, so least privilege and hinges are load-bearing.",
  bridge: "Tools make products capable. Next: structured outputs — the guarantee that Claude's responses arrive as perfectly-shaped JSON your code can trust, every single time.",
  deep: "<p><strong>Tool use: the abstraction that turned language models into software components.</strong> A pure text model is a brilliant conversationalist trapped in a box — it can reason about the world but not touch it, and it can't reliably do what computers do best (exact arithmetic, precise lookups, deterministic operations). Tool use dissolves the box by inverting control: instead of the model BEING the whole system, it becomes the REASONING CORE that orchestrates deterministic tools — delegating math to calculators, facts to databases, actions to APIs. This is the architectural insight behind every serious 2026 AI system, and it resolves the model's deepest weaknesses (hallucination, arithmetic, staleness) not by making the model perfect but by letting it CALL things that are. The pattern has a name gaining currency — the model as «reasoning engine + tool orchestrator» — and it reframes what you're building: not «an AI that knows everything» (impossible, 1.6) but «an AI that knows WHEN TO LOOK THINGS UP and HOW TO ACT» (achievable, and far more useful). Anthropic's «building effective agents» guidance (10.2) is essentially a taxonomy of tool-use patterns. The frontier question — how much autonomy to grant the loop (Track 10) — is entirely a question about tools: which ones, how constrained, with what oversight. And the career shadow is bright: «can design and secure the tool layer of an AI product» is a scarce, senior, well-paid skill (13.1's premium listings), because it sits exactly where AI capability meets real-world consequence — the place businesses most need expertise and most fear getting wrong. You now build there.</p>"
};

/* ------------------------------------------------ 9.5 */
L["9.5"] = {
  sub: "Structured outputs: the guarantee that Claude's response arrives as valid, schema-conforming JSON — turning «usually parseable» into «always parseable», which is the difference between a demo and a product.",
  breath: "Structured outputs force Claude's response to match a JSON schema you define — guaranteed valid, guaranteed shaped — so your code can trust it without defensive parsing; but the schema guarantees form, never truth, so your contracts (null-honesty, totality) still matter.",
  asOf: true,
  hook: "<p>Here's a bug that has broken more AI products than any other: the demo worked, then in production Claude occasionally wrapped its JSON in «Here's the data you requested:» or invented a field name or returned a subtly malformed object — and the code that parsed it crashed, silently, on 1% of requests. Structured outputs kill this entire bug class. They're the unglamorous feature that separates «cool prototype» from «thing you can actually build a business on», and this lesson is short because the idea is clean — but its importance is enormous.</p>",
  secs: [
    { h: "The problem, then the guarantee",
      b: "<p>In chat, you asked for JSON with format instructions (2.4) and usually got it — «usually» being the operative, product-killing word. At scale, «usually valid» means «regularly broken»: one malformed response in a batch of 10,000 is a crashed pipeline or a corrupted record. <strong>Structured outputs</strong> make schema-conformance a GUARANTEE, not a hope:</p>" + PB("structured.py — a guaranteed shape", "response = client.messages.create(\n    model=\"claude-sonnet-5\",\n    max_tokens=500,\n    messages=[{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": invoice_text}],\n    output_config={\"format\": {   # force the shape\n        \"type\": \"json_schema\",\n        \"schema\": {\n            \"type\": \"object\",\n            \"properties\": {\n                \"vendor\": {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                \"amount\": {\"type\": \"number\"},\n                \"date\":   {\"type\": \"string\"},\n                \"status\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"enum\": [\"ok\", \"unreadable\"]},\n            },\n            \"required\": [\"vendor\", \"amount\", \"status\"],\n        },\n    }},\n)\n# The response is GUARANTEED to match this schema. No defensive parsing.", "prompt") + "<p>Now your code can do <code>data = json.loads(response...)</code> and TRUST it — right keys, right types, valid enum values, every time. The «Here's your data:» preamble can't happen; the invented field can't happen; the type drift can't happen. (Exact API shape evolves — verify current syntax in docs — but the guarantee is the durable idea.)</p>"},
    { h: "What it guarantees — and what it emphatically does not",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>Guarantees (form):</strong> valid JSON, correct structure, correct types, enum constraints honoured, required fields present. Syntax risk → zero. This is real and load-bearing.</li><li><strong>Does NOT guarantee (truth):</strong> a schema-valid response can still contain a GUESSED amount, a wrong vendor, a hallucinated value. <code>{&quot;amount&quot;: 5000}</code> is perfectly valid JSON and perfectly wrong if the model invented 5000. <strong>Structure is not correctness.</strong></li><li><strong>So your contracts still rule</strong> (3.3, 9.3): null-honesty («unreadable → null, never guess») must still be in your PROMPT — the schema allows null; your instructions demand it be used honestly. Totality (one output per input) is your pipeline's job. Sampling and reconciliation (5.3) still catch the truth-errors structure can't. <em>Structured outputs remove the syntax-error class entirely, leaving you to focus fully on the truth-error class — which was always the harder, more important one.</em></li></ul>"},
    { h: "Why this makes products possible",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>Composability:</strong> guaranteed-shape outputs snap into pipelines (3.1's chains, coded) — step 2 can rely on step 1's structure, no defensive glue between stages. This is what makes multi-step AI systems maintainable.</li><li><strong>Database-safety:</strong> outputs that go straight into typed database columns need guaranteed types — structured outputs are what let AI write to real schemas without corruption.</li><li><strong>The relationship to tool use</strong> (9.4): tool INPUT schemas already guarantee valid tool calls (strict mode) — structured OUTPUTS extend the same guarantee to Claude's final answer. Both are «the model must produce this shape», enforced at the decoding level.</li><li><strong>The mental shift:</strong> stop writing defensive parsing code that handles Claude's «creativity»; start declaring the shape you need and building on the guarantee. Less code, fewer bugs, real products.</li></ul>"}
  ],
  worked: { t: "Worked example — the pipeline that stopped crashing at 3am",
    html: "<div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>Priya's invoice pipeline (9.3), pre-structured-outputs:</em> worked in testing, then in production it crashed every few hundred invoices — Claude would occasionally return «I found the following:» before the JSON, or format amount as «₹5,000» (a string with a symbol) instead of a number, and her parser died. Silent failures, corrupted rows, 3am debugging. <em>The «usually valid» tax, paid in reliability.</em></p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The fix:</em> she adds the output schema — vendor/amount(number)/date/status(enum). Now EVERY response is valid JSON with a numeric amount and a legal status. The preamble bug: gone. The type-drift bug: gone. The invented-field bug: gone. Her parser deletes 40 lines of defensive «try to clean up whatever Claude returned» code. <em>The syntax-error class vanished entirely.</em></p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The lesson she internalises — and it's the crucial one:</em> the pipeline stopped CRASHING, but she keeps every truth-check (null-honesty in the prompt, reconciliation, sampling) because structure was never her real risk — a guaranteed-valid <code>{&quot;amount&quot;: 5000}</code> is still wrong if 5000 was guessed. <em>Structured outputs let her stop worrying about FORM and focus entirely on TRUTH — which is where the hard, valuable engineering always was.</em> The feature that seems boring is the one that made the product real.</p></div>"},
  lab: { where: "Claude Code",
    html: "<p>Upgrade your 9.3 vision pipeline (or 9.4 assistant) with structured outputs and feel the reliability shift:</p>" + PB("prompt — add the guarantee", "Take my extraction pipeline and add structured outputs: define a JSON schema for the output (fields: [YOURS], with correct types and an enum for status: ok/unreadable). Enforce it via the API's structured output feature so every response is guaranteed to match. Then: (1) remove any defensive JSON-cleaning code we no longer need; (2) run it on my test documents INCLUDING a deliberately messy/ambiguous one; (3) confirm every response parsed cleanly with json.loads and no try/except gymnastics. IMPORTANT: keep the null-honesty rule in the PROMPT — remind me why the schema alone doesn't guarantee honest values.", "prompt") + "<p>Note what got deleted (defensive parsing) and what stayed (truth rules). That division is the lesson.</p>",
    expect: "A pipeline that parses cleanly every time, minus the defensive cruft — plus a clear-eyed understanding that the schema fixed FORM and your prompt/sampling still guard TRUTH. This is the reliability foundation every real AI product stands on." },
  mistakes: [
    "<b>Believing schema-valid = correct.</b> The #1 dangerous misunderstanding. A guaranteed-shape response can hold guessed, wrong values. Structure ≠ truth; keep your truth stack (3.4/9.3).",
    "<b>Dropping null-honesty because «the schema handles it».</b> The schema ALLOWS null; your prompt must DEMAND it for unreadable fields. Otherwise you get valid JSON full of confident guesses.",
    "<b>Still writing defensive parsing «just in case».</b> The guarantee is real — trust it and delete the cruft. Belt-and-suspenders here is just belt-and-belt.",
    "<b>Over-constraining schemas.</b> An enum that's missing a real category forces the model into a wrong bucket — design schemas to include an «other»/«unknown» escape (the never-guess principle, schema edition).",
    "<b>Ignoring it and shipping «usually valid» to production.</b> The bug will find you at scale, at 3am. Structured outputs exist precisely so it doesn't."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "Structured outputs guarantee:",
      opts: ["That the values are correct", "That the response matches your schema — valid JSON, right structure, right types, valid enums — eliminating the syntax-error class entirely", "That Claude is smarter", "Nothing useful"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Emphatically not — form, never truth. A valid schema can hold a wrong number.", "Correct — syntax risk goes to zero, which is the difference between a demo and a product. The truth-error class remains yours.", "Same model; the decoding is constrained to your shape.", "Eliminating a whole bug class is enormously useful."] },
    { q: "Why must null-honesty stay in the PROMPT even with a schema that allows null?",
      opts: ["It doesn't — the schema handles it", "The schema ALLOWS null but doesn't DEMAND honest use of it — without the prompt rule, the model returns valid JSON full of guessed values instead of honest nulls", "Prompts are decorative now", "For legal reasons"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["The schema permits null; it doesn't require the model to be honest about when to use it.", "Correct — form vs truth, precisely. `{amount: 5000}` is valid whether 5000 is read or invented; the prompt is what makes the model write null when it can't read (3.3's contract, still essential).", "Prompts carry the truth discipline structure can't.", "It's about correctness, not compliance."] },
    { q: "The main reason structured outputs make multi-step AI pipelines maintainable:",
      opts: ["They're faster", "Guaranteed-shape outputs snap into the next stage with no defensive glue — step 2 can rely on step 1's structure, so chains (3.1) become reliable code", "They use less memory", "They're required by law"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Speed isn't the point.", "Correct — composability: each stage trusts the previous stage's shape. This is what turns 3.1's hand-run chains into robust automated pipelines.", "Marginal.", "They're an engineering choice, and the right one for products."] }
  ],
  prac: [
    { tag: "warm", q: "A schema requires status to be one of [approved, rejected]. A real case is genuinely ambiguous. What's the design flaw, and the fix?",
      sol: "<b>Flaw:</b> the enum forces a wrong bucket — the model must pick approved or rejected even when neither is true, manufacturing false confidence (the over-constrained-schema mistake). <b>Fix:</b> add «needs_review» or «unclear» to the enum — give the never-guess principle a legal home in the schema. Schemas should always include an escape hatch for honest uncertainty, exactly like 2.2's UNCLEAR label." },
    { tag: "transfer", q: "Add structured outputs to a real pipeline, then deliberately test the form/truth distinction: find (or construct) a case where the output is schema-valid but the VALUE is wrong. How would you catch it, since the schema can't?",
      sol: "<b>Catching truth-errors (since schema won't):</b> reconciliation (does the aggregate match a control total?), sampling (spot-check values against sources — 5.3), cross-field validation in your code (does date < today? is amount within plausible range?), and confidence/null flags routing doubtful cases to humans. The schema deleted your syntax worries so you can spend ALL your verification effort here — on truth, where it always mattered most (3.4)." },
    { tag: "stretch", q: "Explain how structured outputs, tool use (9.4), and extraction contracts (3.3) relate — they're three views of one deeper idea. What is it, and why does grasping it make you a better builder?",
      sol: "<b>The deeper idea:</b> CONSTRAINING GENERATION TO A REQUIRED SHAPE. Tool-input schemas constrain what Claude can request; structured outputs constrain what Claude can answer; extraction contracts constrain (via prompt) what Claude should produce. All three narrow the model's open-ended generation to a shape your system can rely on — some enforced at the decoder level (structured outputs, strict tool schemas — the model literally cannot emit off-shape tokens), some at the prompt level (contracts — the model is instructed and usually complies). <b>Why it matters:</b> building reliable AI systems is largely the art of CONSTRAINT — you get the model's intelligence AND your system's guarantees by shaping the generation, not by hoping. The builder who thinks in constraints (what shape do I need, and how do I enforce it — decoder-level where possible, prompt-level where not, verification always) ships products; the one who thinks in hopes ships demos. This constraint-thinking is the through-line from Track 3's contracts to Track 10's agent guardrails — one idea, rising in stakes." }
  ],
  recap: "Structured outputs guarantee Claude's response matches your JSON schema — killing the syntax-error class and freeing you to focus entirely on truth-errors, which your prompt contracts and sampling still guard; structure is form, never correctness.",
  review: "From Lesson 3.3: extraction contracts made output «mostly» right by instruction; structured outputs make its SHAPE guaranteed by the decoder — the contract's form-half, mechanised, leaving its truth-half fully to you.",
  bridge: "Your products are capable and reliable. Now make them PROFITABLE: caching and batching — the features that cut costs by up to 90% and turn thin-margin ideas into real businesses.",
  deep: "<p><strong>Constrained decoding: how the guarantee actually works.</strong> Structured outputs aren't «Claude tries harder to make valid JSON» — they're grammar-constrained decoding: at each generation step, the system computes which next tokens are LEGAL given the schema (after `{&quot;amount&quot;:` only a number can follow, not «₹» or a letter) and masks all illegal tokens to zero probability before sampling. The model literally CANNOT emit a schema-violating token — validity is mechanical, not probabilistic. This is the same technique behind strict tool-input schemas (9.4) and, conceptually, behind stop sequences (9.2). Two deep implications. First, it reveals a general principle: you can impose HARD guarantees on a probabilistic system by constraining its output space at the decoder — the model's intelligence chooses AMONG legal options; your schema defines what's legal. This is the cleanest example of the deterministic-guardrails-around-probabilistic-core pattern (7.5's two-material engineering) — the constraint is absolute, the intelligence operates within it. Second, it clarifies the permanent division of labour that all of Track 9 has been teaching: MECHANISM guarantees form (constrained decoding, tool execution, code for math), INTELLIGENCE provides judgment (what to extract, which tool, what it means), and VERIFICATION guards truth (sampling, reconciliation) because neither mechanism nor intelligence guarantees correctness. A builder who internalises this trinity — constrain what you can, delegate what's deterministic, verify what remains — builds systems that are simultaneously flexible and trustworthy, which is the entire art. The industry converged here because it's the only way to get both: pure prompting is flexible but unreliable; pure code is reliable but rigid; the composite is what ships. You now think in the composite.</p>"
};

/* ------------------------------------------------ 9.6 */
L["9.6"] = {
  sub: "Prompt caching and batch processing: the two features that cut costs by up to 90% — turning ideas that «don't quite pencil out» into profitable products.",
  breath: "Prompt caching stores a reused prefix (system prompt, documents, examples) so repeat calls pay ~10% for it instead of full price; the Batch API processes non-urgent requests asynchronously at 50% off — together they're the economics that decide whether a product makes money.",
  asOf: true,
  hook: "<p>Two products, identical features, identical quality. One is wildly profitable; the other loses money on every user. The difference isn't the AI — it's whether the builder understood caching and batching. These two features routinely cut real costs by 50–90%, and they're the reason «this idea is too expensive to run» becomes «this idea prints money». Non-glamorous, decisive, and skipped by most beginners — which is exactly why knowing them is an edge. This is the lesson where AI engineering becomes AI BUSINESS.</p>",
  secs: [
    { h: "Prompt caching: stop paying for what doesn't change",
      b: "<p>Recall statelessness (9.2): every call resends the whole context — and you PAY to reprocess it every time. But much of it never changes: your big system prompt, the reference documents, the few-shot examples. Caching stops the repeat-payment:</p><ul><li><strong>How it works:</strong> mark a stable prefix as cacheable. The first call processes and caches it (a small write premium, ~1.25×). Subsequent calls that reuse that exact prefix pay ~0.1× (10%) for the cached part instead of full price. Cache lives ~5 minutes (refreshed on use) or ~1 hour (higher write cost, longer life).</li><li><strong>The economics:</strong> a 10,000-token system prompt + examples, reused across 1,000 calls: without caching you pay for 10M tokens of prefix; with caching, ~1M-ish. On a big stable prefix, <strong>90% of that cost evaporates.</strong></li><li><strong>The design rule (and why 3.2's ordering mattered):</strong> put STABLE content FIRST (system, documents, examples), VARIABLE content LAST (the user's specific query) — so the cacheable prefix is maximal. This is EXACTLY the docs-top-task-bottom layout (3.2) — you learned the cache-optimal structure before you knew caching existed. Caching pays after ~1 reuse (5-min) or ~2 (1-hour) — so it's for repeated-prefix workloads, which is most products.</li></ul>"},
    { h: "Batch processing: half price for patience",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>The deal:</strong> submit many requests as a batch, get results back asynchronously (most within an hour, up to 24), pay <strong>50% less</strong> on both input and output. Half price, for not needing it THIS second.</li><li><strong>What it's for:</strong> anything not real-time — processing a document backlog (9.3), classifying 50,000 records, generating content in bulk, running evals (3.6), overnight analysis. If a human isn't watching a spinner, batch it.</li><li><strong>The pattern:</strong> real-time user requests → normal API (streaming, instant); bulk/background work → Batch API (async, 50% off). Most products have both, and routing work to the right lane is free money.</li><li><strong>Scale:</strong> huge batches (tens of thousands of requests) in one submission — the infrastructure for turning «process everything» from a cost fear into a line item.</li></ul>"},
    { h: "Stacking them into product economics",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>They compose:</strong> a batch job with a cached prefix gets BOTH discounts — 50% off the batch, 90% off the repeated prefix. Priya's 50,000-invoice monthly run, batched with her cached extraction prompt, might cost a fraction of the naive price.</li><li><strong>Plus model mix</strong> (1.3): Haiku for the volume, escalate only where needed — the three levers (right model + caching + batching) together can be the difference between 10× cost and 1× cost on the same workload.</li><li><strong>Why this IS the business:</strong> in AI products, gross margin lives here. A support tool that costs ₹2/conversation at ₹20 revenue is a business; the same tool naively built at ₹15/conversation is a charity. The features are unglamorous; the margin they create is the whole game (9.7 makes it arithmetic).</li><li><strong>Verify current pricing</strong> (multipliers and cache durations evolve — docs are truth) — but the PRINCIPLES (don't repay for stable context; trade latency for discount) are permanent economics.</li></ul>"}
  ],
  worked: { t: "Worked example — the idea that only worked after caching",
    html: "<div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The product:</em> an AI tutor that answers student questions grounded in a specific 80-page textbook. <em>Naive build:</em> every question resends the 80 pages (~30,000 tokens) as context. At a few thousand questions a day, the textbook-reprocessing cost alone made the product lose money at any price students would pay. «Too expensive to run» — the idea nearly died here.</p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>Caching saves it:</em> the textbook is the STABLE prefix (it never changes); the student's question is the VARIABLE suffix. Structured docs-first/question-last (3.2 = cache-optimal), the textbook cached, each question now pays ~10% for the 30k-token context instead of full price. <em>The dominant cost fell ~90%.</em> The same product, same quality, flipped from unprofitable to comfortably profitable — by one architectural choice.</p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>Then the bulk side:</em> the nightly job that pre-generates practice quizzes for every chapter? Batched — 50% off, and nobody's waiting. And the whole thing runs on Sonnet with Haiku for the simple lookups (1.3). <em>Three levers, stacked, turned a money-losing idea into a business.</em> The founder's honest reflection: «The AI was never the hard part. The ECONOMICS were — and they're a solved problem if you know these features exist.» That knowledge is this lesson, and it's worth more than most «AI skills» because it's the one that decides if the product lives.</p></div>"},
  lab: { where: "Claude Code",
    html: "<p>Measure caching's effect with real numbers — the most convincing way to learn it:</p>" + PB("prompt — caching experiment", "Build a script that demonstrates prompt caching's cost effect. Set up a call with a LARGE stable prefix (a long system prompt + a big document — a few thousand tokens) and a short variable question at the end (docs-first, question-last ordering). Run it: (1) WITHOUT caching, 5 times with different questions — record total tokens billed; (2) WITH the prefix cached, the same 5 questions — record tokens billed (note the cache write on call 1, then the ~10% reads). Show me both totals and the percentage saved. Then explain: which part of MY prompt should always be cacheable, and where the break-even is.", "prompt") + "<p>Then think about ONE product idea you have: what's its stable prefix, and what would caching + batching do to its unit cost?</p>",
    expect: "Real numbers showing the prefix cost collapse on cached calls — usually a dramatic saving on any large-stable-prefix workload. The visceral «oh, THAT'S how AI products make money» moment. Note the docs-first ordering (3.2) is what makes the prefix maximally cacheable — you built for this before you knew it." },
  mistakes: [
    "<b>Variable content before stable content.</b> Breaks the cacheable prefix — the user's query up top means nothing after it can be cached. Docs-first/query-last (3.2) is cache-optimal AND quality-optimal.",
    "<b>Real-time API for bulk work.</b> Paying double (no batch discount) for work no human is waiting on. Route background jobs to the Batch API — free 50%.",
    "<b>Caching tiny prefixes.</b> Caching has a small write premium; it pays off on LARGE reused prefixes over multiple calls, not on a two-line system prompt. Match the tool to the workload.",
    "<b>Ignoring economics until launch.</b> «We'll optimise later» ships a product that loses money per user — and the fix is architectural (prefix ordering, model mix), best designed in. Cost is a day-one concern (9.7).",
    "<b>Reciting old prices as gospel.</b> Multipliers and durations change — the PRINCIPLES are permanent; verify current numbers in docs before you model a business on them."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "Prompt caching saves money by:",
      opts: ["Making Claude think less", "Not re-charging full price to reprocess the STABLE prefix (system, docs, examples) on repeat calls — cached parts cost ~10% instead of full price", "Using a cheaper model", "Skipping the response"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Same computation, same quality.", "Correct — statelessness means you resend and repay for context every call (9.2); caching stops the repay on the unchanging part. 90% savings on big stable prefixes.", "That's model mix (1.3) — a separate, stackable lever.", "You get the full response — just cheaper on the cached input."] },
    { q: "The docs-first/query-last ordering (3.2) turns out to also be:",
      opts: ["A coincidence", "Cache-optimal — the stable prefix (docs) is maximised for caching, and the variable part (query) is last, so you learned the cost-optimal structure before you knew caching existed", "Slower", "Only about quality"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Deeply not a coincidence — it's the same structural logic.", "Correct — stable-first/variable-last serves attention (3.2) AND cacheability simultaneously. The course's principles keep compounding across tracks.", "It's both faster (cache reads) and cheaper.", "It's about quality AND economics — the same layout serves both."] },
    { q: "The Batch API's trade:",
      opts: ["Worse quality for lower price", "Latency for price — async results (up to ~24h, usually <1h) in exchange for 50% off, ideal for anything no human is waiting on", "Fewer features", "Nothing — it's identical to real-time"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Same models, same quality — only timing differs.", "Correct — trade the spinner nobody's watching for half price. Bulk extraction, classification, evals, overnight jobs.", "Same capabilities, async delivery.", "The difference is timing and price — significant for margins."] }
  ],
  prac: [
    { tag: "warm", q: "For each, say: cache the prefix? batch it? (a) a chatbot answering questions about a fixed 100-page manual, (b) a real-time customer support widget, (c) classifying last month's 40,000 support tickets, (d) a coding assistant with a big system prompt used all day.",
      sol: "(a) <b>cache</b> the manual (stable prefix, reused per question) (b) real-time (streaming), cache the system prompt if large (c) <b>batch</b> — non-urgent bulk, 50% off (d) <b>cache</b> the system prompt (large, reused all day). The pattern: stable+reused → cache; bulk+non-urgent → batch; often both apply." },
    { tag: "transfer", q: "Run the caching experiment lab, then take one real product idea and estimate its unit economics twice: naive vs optimised (right model + caching + batching where applicable). Does the optimisation change whether it's viable?",
      sol: "<b>What you'll typically find:</b> the naive estimate is 5–15× the optimised one on any workload with a large stable prefix or bulk component. For many ideas, naive = unviable and optimised = profitable — which means the economics knowledge is LITERALLY the difference between the business existing or not. This exact calculation is 9.7's whole subject and a core skill you'll sell (11.2/12.2)." },
    { tag: "stretch", q: "A founder says «we'll just raise prices to cover the AI costs.» Explain why understanding these features is usually better than raising prices, using the concept of gross margin and competitive dynamics.",
      sol: "<b>The argument:</b> (1) Gross margin — cutting cost 80% via caching/batching/model-mix improves margin without touching price, so you keep customers AND make money; raising price loses customers at the margin. (2) Competitive dynamics — a competitor who DOES understand these features can price 80% lower than your raised price and still profit, so «raise prices» is a strategy that loses to any cost-literate rival. (3) The features are free (built into the API) and mostly architectural (prefix ordering, lane routing) — leaving them on the table is unforced margin destruction. (4) There's a floor case where price MUST rise (genuinely expensive per-unit value), but you only know you're at it AFTER optimising. <b>The deeper point:</b> in a market where everyone has the same model at the same list price (1.3), COST ENGINEERING is a durable competitive advantage — the builders who master caching, batching, and model mix out-margin and out-price those who don't. This is why 'boring' economics is a moat, and why this unglamorous lesson may be the most commercially valuable in the track.</p>" }
  ],
  recap: "Caching stops repaying for the stable prefix (~90% off large reused context, and docs-first ordering makes it cache-optimal); batching trades latency for 50% off bulk work; stacked with model mix, they turn unprofitable ideas into businesses — cost engineering is a durable moat.",
  review: "From Lesson 3.2: docs-top/task-bottom served attention and grounding — it turns out to be the cache-optimal layout too. And from 1.3: model mix is the third lever that stacks with these two. The course's principles were always economics in disguise.",
  bridge: "You can build capable, reliable, cheap products. The track's synthesis: cost engineering as a discipline, and then shipping a complete mini-product end to end — proving you can do the whole thing.",
  deep: "<p><strong>Why cost structure IS strategy in the application layer.</strong> When the underlying model is a commodity — same Claude, same list prices, available to every competitor (9.1's deep-dive) — you cannot win on model quality alone; everyone has the identical engine. Competition shifts to what you build AROUND the model, and a huge, underappreciated part of that is UNIT ECONOMICS. Consider the structure: your cost per user is roughly (tokens per interaction × price per token × interactions), and every term is a lever — caching slashes the token count you pay for, batching halves the price on bulk, model mix optimises price-per-token by task, prompt design reduces tokens per interaction (2.2's brevity, now a cost control), and product design reduces unnecessary interactions. A builder who pulls all these levers can offer the same product at a fraction of a naive competitor's cost — enabling lower prices (winning customers), higher margins (funding growth), or both. This is why «AI cost optimization» is emerging as a real 2026 discipline and job function, and why FinOps-for-AI is a line item in enterprise budgets: at scale, a 30% cost improvement is worth more than most feature launches. The strategic reframe for you: the «boring» economics lessons aren't secondary to the «exciting» capability lessons — they're where the moat and the money actually are, precisely BECAUSE they're unglamorous and skipped. Your competitors are dazzled by capabilities; you understand costs. In a commodity-model world, that's the durable edge — and it's a skill you can sell directly (9.7 → 11/12): «I'll cut your AI bill 70% without touching quality» is a pitch that closes itself, because it's pure found money for the client. The most valuable AI skill is often not making it work, but making it work profitably.</p>"
};

/* ------------------------------------------------ 9.7 */
L["9.7"] = {
  sub: "Cost engineering as a discipline: model mix, token budgets, and the unit-economics math that decides whether any AI product makes money — the skill that pays for itself.",
  breath: "Cost engineering is deliberate design for margin — routing each task to the cheapest capable model, minimising tokens per interaction, applying caching and batching, and modelling cost-per-user against price — turning «will this make money?» from a hope into a calculation.",
  asOf: true,
  hook: "<p>Every AI product lives or dies on one number: cost per user versus revenue per user. Get it right and you have a business that scales beautifully; get it wrong and every new user makes you poorer. This lesson turns the scattered cost levers (models, tokens, caching, batching) into a single discipline you can APPLY to any idea and, crucially, SELL to any company — because «make our AI profitable» is one of the most valuable and least-supplied services in the 2026 market. Cost engineering is where AI skill becomes AI money most directly.</p>",
  secs: [
    { h: "The unit-economics equation",
      b: "<p>Every AI product's viability reduces to this:</p><div class='callout gold'><b>Profit per user = Revenue per user − Cost per user</b><br>where <b>Cost per user ≈ (interactions × tokens-per-interaction × effective-price-per-token)</b></div><p>Cost engineering is pulling every term DOWN without hurting the product:</p><ul><li><strong>interactions</strong> ↓ — product design: solve the user's need in fewer round-trips (a good first answer beats five clarifying ones).</li><li><strong>tokens-per-interaction</strong> ↓ — prompt design (2.2's brevity is now a cost control), context management (9.2's memory strategy), output bounds (max_tokens).</li><li><strong>effective-price-per-token</strong> ↓ — the big one: model mix (1.3), caching (9.6, ~90% off stable prefix), batching (9.6, 50% off bulk). «Effective» because these can put your real price-per-token far below any single model's list price.</li></ul><p>Model this BEFORE building (a spreadsheet, 20 minutes) and you know if the idea pencils out — turning «I hope this is profitable» into «at ₹X/user cost and ₹Y price, gross margin is Z%.»</p>"},
    { h: "The model-mix architecture",
      b: "<p>The highest-leverage cost move is refusing to use one model for everything (1.3's law, now architecture):</p><ul><li><strong>Route by task difficulty:</strong> a cheap classifier (Haiku) triages incoming work; simple cases → Haiku answers; complex cases → Sonnet or flagship. Most real workloads are 80% simple / 20% hard — so routing puts 80% of volume on the cheapest model.</li><li><strong>Cascade:</strong> try the cheap model first; escalate ONLY when its answer fails a confidence or quality check (1.3's escalation, automated). Pay premium prices only for the fraction that needs them.</li><li><strong>Right-size per stage</strong> (3.1's chains): in a pipeline, each step gets its own model — Haiku extracts, Sonnet analyses, flagship writes the one high-stakes artifact. Never pay flagship prices for extraction.</li><li><strong>The savings compound:</strong> model mix (5–12× on the routed portion) × caching (up to 90% on stable prefix) × batching (50% on bulk) can stack to an order-of-magnitude total cost difference on the same workload at the same quality.</li></ul>"},
    { h: "Cost engineering as a sellable service",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>The pitch that closes itself:</strong> «Your AI feature costs ₹X/user; I'll cut it 60–80% without touching quality — via model mix, caching, batching, and prompt optimisation — for a fee that's a fraction of the annual saving.» Pure found money for the client; easy yes.</li><li><strong>The audit deliverable:</strong> instrument their AI calls (what model, how many tokens, cached?), find the waste (flagship-for-everything, no caching, real-time bulk, bloated prompts), model the optimised architecture, quantify the saving. This IS a consulting engagement (11.2, dev tier) and a genuinely scarce skill.</li><li><strong>Guardrails you install</strong> (1.5, now product-grade): spending limits, per-user rate caps, cost monitoring/alerts, a kill-switch — so a bug or abuse can't produce a surprise bill. Companies pay for peace of mind here alone.</li><li><strong>The honest caveat:</strong> optimise for the RIGHT metric — sometimes the flagship's better answer drives more revenue than its extra cost, so «cheapest» ≠ «most profitable». Cost engineering serves margin, not miserliness; the goal is profit-per-user, not minimum-cost-per-user.</li></ul>"}
  ],
  worked: { t: "Worked example — the audit that saved a startup ₹4 lakh/month",
    html: "<div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The client:</em> a startup with an AI feature (document Q&A) bleeding money — CEO says «the AI costs are eating our margin, is the whole idea dead?» <em>The audit (a day's work):</em> instrument the calls. Findings: they use the flagship for EVERYTHING (including trivial lookups), never cache the documents (re-sent full price every question), run their nightly report-generation on the real-time API, and their prompts carry 2,000 tokens of redundant boilerplate.</p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The optimised architecture:</em> (1) route — Haiku for simple lookups (80% of volume), Sonnet for complex synthesis, flagship reserved for the rare hard case: ~7× cheaper on the routed majority. (2) Cache the documents (stable prefix, docs-first ordering): ~85% off the dominant context cost. (3) Batch the nightly reports: 50% off. (4) Trim the boilerplate: 2,000 tokens → 300. <em>Stacked, the per-user cost fell ~75%.</em></p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The result:</em> ₹4 lakh/month saved, the feature flipped from margin-killer to profitable, the «is the idea dead?» question answered with «no — it was just built naively.» <em>The consultant's fee:</em> a fraction of the first month's saving — an instant, obvious yes for the client. <em>The meta-lesson:</em> the AI worked fine all along; the ECONOMICS were broken, and fixing economics is a distinct, scarce, well-paid skill that most «AI people» don't have because they were dazzled by capabilities. You now have it — and it's one of the straightest lines from this course to income.</em></p></div>"},
  lab: { where: "Claude Code + a spreadsheet",
    html: "<p>Build a unit-economics model for a real product idea (yours, or analyse an existing AI feature). Commission the analysis:</p>" + PB("prompt — unit economics model", "Help me build a unit-economics model for this AI product idea: [DESCRIBE IT — what it does, how often a user interacts, roughly how many tokens per interaction]. Build a spreadsheet/calculation showing cost-per-user under two architectures: (1) NAIVE — one flagship model for everything, no caching, no batching; (2) OPTIMISED — model mix (Haiku/Sonnet/flagship routed by task), caching the stable prefix, batching any bulk work, trimmed prompts. Use current API prices (look them up — verify, don't guess). Show me: cost-per-user both ways, the % saved, and — given a plausible price I could charge — the gross margin under each. Then tell me: is this idea viable, and which lever mattered most?", "prompt") + "<p>The output tells you whether your idea is a business — and if not naively, whether cost engineering makes it one.</p>",
    expect: "A real cost-per-user number, a naive-vs-optimised comparison (usually a dramatic gap), and a viability verdict grounded in arithmetic instead of hope. This model IS the core of a business plan AND a client deliverable — you've just done the calculation that most AI founders skip and most AI failures needed." },
  mistakes: [
    "<b>Not modelling economics before building.</b> Discovering per-user losses at scale, when the fix is architectural and should've been designed in. The 20-minute spreadsheet prevents the doomed build.",
    "<b>Optimising cost at the expense of profit.</b> Sometimes the pricier model's better answer earns more than it costs — optimise for margin/profit-per-user, not minimum cost. Miserliness isn't the goal.",
    "<b>Flagship-for-everything as a default.</b> The single most common, most expensive architectural sin (1.3's mirror). Route by difficulty; reserve premium for what needs it.",
    "<b>No cost guardrails in production.</b> No spending limit, no rate caps, no alerts — one bug or one abuser produces a nightmare bill. Guardrails are day-one infrastructure (1.5, product edition).",
    "<b>Reciting stale prices.</b> Model this on CURRENT numbers (verify in docs) — prices and discounts shift, and a business modelled on last year's rates is modelled wrong."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "The unit-economics equation for an AI product is essentially:",
      opts: ["Revenue minus salaries", "Profit-per-user = revenue-per-user − (interactions × tokens-per-interaction × effective-price-per-token)", "Just the model's list price", "Impossible to estimate"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Salaries matter for the whole business, but per-USER AI cost is the equation that decides scalable viability.", "Correct — every term is a lever cost engineering pulls down. Model this before building and you know if the idea pencils out.", "«Effective» price ≠ list price — caching/batching/mix put it far lower; that's the whole discipline.", "It's a 20-minute spreadsheet — and skipping it is why AI products fail."] },
    { q: "The highest-leverage single cost move is usually:",
      opts: ["Using the newest model", "Model mix — routing 80% of (typically simple) volume to the cheapest capable model and reserving premium models for the hard 20%", "Turning off the AI", "Charging more"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Newest often means priciest — the opposite of cost engineering.", "Correct — most workloads are 80% simple; routing that majority to Haiku is the biggest lever, stacking with caching and batching. Never one model for everything.", "That's not a product.", "Raising prices loses customers to cost-literate rivals (9.6's argument)."] },
    { q: "Why is cost engineering a scarce, sellable skill?",
      opts: ["It's not — everyone knows it", "Most «AI people» focus on capabilities and skip economics, so «cut your AI bill 60-80% without hurting quality» is found money for clients and a rare service — an easy yes", "It requires a PhD", "Clients don't care about costs"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Demonstrably scarce — most AI failures died of unmodelled economics.", "Correct — the pitch closes itself because it's pure saving for the client, and few can deliver it. The ₹4-lakh audit is the template.", "It requires the levers in this track — which you now have — not a PhD.", "At scale, AI cost is often a top-3 line item — they care intensely."] }
  ],
  prac: [
    { tag: "warm", q: "A product does 10 interactions/user/day, ~1,000 tokens each, on the flagship (~$10/$50 per MTok, say ~$30/MTok blended). Rough monthly cost per user? Then: what's the biggest lever to cut it?",
      sol: "<b>Rough math:</b> 10 × 1,000 × 30 days = 300,000 tokens/user/month ≈ 0.3 MTok × ~$30 ≈ ~$9/user/month. <b>Biggest lever:</b> model mix — if 80% of interactions are simple and go to Haiku (~$2/MTok blended), the blended price-per-token drops ~5×, taking cost toward ~$2–3/user. Then cache any stable prefix and trim tokens for more. At $9/user you need high revenue; at $2–3 far more ideas pencil out. (Numbers illustrative — verify current prices.)" },
    { tag: "transfer", q: "Build the full unit-economics lab on a real idea, then write the one-paragraph verdict a founder needs: is it viable, at what price, at what margin, and which lever was decisive?",
      sol: "<b>The verdict's shape:</b> «At optimised cost of ₹X/user and a plausible price of ₹Y, gross margin is Z%. Viable [yes/no/only-if]. The decisive lever was [model mix / caching / interactions], contributing N% of the saving.» This paragraph is simultaneously your go/no-go decision AND the executive summary of a client cost-audit deliverable (11.2). One calculation, two uses." },
    { tag: "stretch", q: "Design the complete «AI cost audit» service you could sell: the process, the deliverable, the pricing model, and how you'd guarantee value to make it a no-risk yes for the client.",
      sol: "<b>Process:</b> instrument their calls (models, tokens, caching, cadence) → find waste → model optimised architecture → quantify saving → implement or hand off. <b>Deliverable:</b> current-state cost breakdown, optimised architecture, projected saving with confidence range, implementation plan, and installed guardrails (limits, alerts, kill-switch). <b>Pricing:</b> the powerful move — performance-based, e.g., fixed fee + a share of the first N months' verified savings, so the client pays largely FROM the money you save them. <b>Guarantee:</b> «if I don't find at least X% savings, the audit is free» — de-risks it to a trivial yes, and you only offer it because you KNOW the waste is nearly always there (flagship-for-everything + no caching is epidemic). <b>Why it works:</b> found money, scarce skill, self-funding fee, guaranteed downside — this is close to an unlosable service, and it exists BECAUSE most builders skip this track's economics. You didn't. The lesson that looked like accounting was a business.</p>" }
  ],
  recap: "Cost engineering is deliberate design for margin — route by difficulty (model mix), minimise tokens and interactions, stack caching and batching, and model cost-per-user vs price before building; it's a scarce, self-funding, sellable discipline because most builders skip it.",
  review: "From Lesson 1.3 and 9.6: model selection and caching/batching were introduced as features; here they compose into a DISCIPLINE and a business — the course's economics thread reaching its point.",
  bridge: "You understand the whole API — capability, reliability, economics. The track's finale: build a complete mini-product end to end, proving you can take an idea from prompt to shipped, profitable thing.",
  deep: "<p><strong>Cost engineering and the shape of the AI economy.</strong> Zoom out: the AI value chain has layers — foundation models (Anthropic, a few labs; capital-intensive, few winners), infrastructure (clouds, tooling), and the APPLICATION layer (products solving specific problems; where most businesses and most builders live). Foundation-model economics are brutal and concentrated; application economics are where individual opportunity concentrates, and they hinge precisely on the discipline this lesson taught — because at the application layer, the model is a commodity input whose LIST price everyone pays equally, so your margin comes from how EFFICIENTLY you use it. This creates a durable, slightly counterintuitive truth: in the application layer, operational excellence (cost engineering, reliability, prompt efficiency) often matters MORE than novel capability, because capability is rented and identical across competitors while operations are yours. It's the same reason low-cost operators win commoditised industries (airlines, retail, cloud itself) — when the product is similar, the efficient operator wins on margin and can then win on price. The 2026 job market reflects this: «AI engineer» increasingly means someone who builds RELIABLE, EFFICIENT systems on commodity models, not someone who trains models (a rare, different, PhD-heavy role) — and the efficiency half is exactly what most self-taught «prompt people» lack, making it your differentiator and your premium. The deepest career insight of this track: you don't need to build models to profit from AI (almost nobody does); you need to build efficient, reliable, valuable PRODUCTS AND SYSTEMS on top of them — and the economics discipline is what makes those products businesses rather than expensive hobbies. The money tracks (11-13) monetise this every way — services (audit their costs), products (your margins), and jobs (the premium skill) — but they all rest on the calculation you just learned to do. The unglamorous spreadsheet is the foundation of the exciting business.</p>"
};

/* ------------------------------------------------ 9.8 */
L["9.8"] = {
  sub: "Ship it: build a complete mini-product end to end — idea to running, reliable, cost-aware thing — proving you can do the whole loop, and creating a portfolio piece that argues for you.",
  breath: "The track's synthesis: take one real idea through the full build — prompt design, API integration, tool use or vision, structured outputs, cost engineering, verification, and a usable interface — shipping a small but complete product that demonstrates every skill at once.",
  hook: "<p>Knowledge you can't assemble is trivia. This lesson refuses to let that happen: you'll take everything — Track 2's prompts, Track 3's contracts, Track 7's build discipline, Track 8's security, this track's API — and ship ONE complete mini-product. Not a tutorial toy: a small, real, working thing that reads or does something useful, reliably, at a cost that makes sense, with an interface a human can use. When you finish, you won't just KNOW the API — you'll have PROOF you can build with it, which is worth more than any certificate in the rooms where money changes hands (Track 13 will confirm it).</p>",
  secs: [
    { h: "What «complete» means (the shipping checklist)",
      b: "<p>A mini-product isn't a script — it's a small system with every layer present. Your build must have:</p><ul><li><strong>A real job:</strong> it solves an actual problem for a real user (even if that user is you). «A tool that turns my meeting recordings into action-item emails» beats «a demo of the API».</li><li><strong>Sound prompt engineering</strong> (Tracks 2–3): system prompt as the product's soul (2.6), format contracts (2.4), truth rules (3.4) — the intelligence layer, done right.</li><li><strong>The right API features</strong> (this track): tool use if it acts, vision if it reads documents, structured outputs for anything your code parses, streaming if a human watches.</li><li><strong>Reliability</strong> (3.4, 9.5): verification where it matters, honest failure (never fabricate), errors handled — it works on the messy real input, not just the happy path.</li><li><strong>Cost-awareness</strong> (9.6–9.7): right model(s), caching if there's a stable prefix, a spending guardrail — it's economically sane.</li><li><strong>An interface:</strong> something a human uses — a simple web page (an artifact! 4.5, or a small HTML front-end Claude Code builds), a CLI, a form. The AI is the engine; the interface is the product.</li><li><strong>Shippability</strong> (7.8): README, secrets clean, in a repo — a stranger could run it. This is the portfolio-piece standard.</li></ul>"},
    { h: "The build loop (7.8, applied to a product)",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>PLAN:</strong> pick the idea (small enough to finish, real enough to matter), name its user and its one job, sketch the architecture (what models, what tools/vision, what the interface is), and — critically — the verification plan and cost model (9.7) BEFORE building.</li><li><strong>BUILD with Claude Code</strong> (Track 7): scaffold, integrate the API, build the interface, in reviewed small steps on a branch. The agent writes the code; you direct and verify — the whole builder phase, applied.</li><li><strong>VERIFY:</strong> run it on real, messy input including edge cases; confirm the verification and honest-failure behaviour; check the cost is sane; make sure it works for someone who isn't you.</li><li><strong>SHIP:</strong> README, secrets audit, commit, push. Optionally deploy (a static front-end + the API can go live cheaply). Then USE it for real — the ultimate test.</li></ul>"},
    { h: "Why this matters more than it seems",
      b: "<ul><li><strong>Integration is the real skill.</strong> Knowing each feature separately is necessary; assembling them into a working whole is the actual job — and the thing employers and clients actually test for (13.5's interviews are «show me something you built»).</li><li><strong>The portfolio argument</strong> (7.8, 13.3): a repo of small, real, working AI products is 2026's most credible resume — undeniable proof, not claimed skills. This build is its first serious entry.</li><li><strong>The product could be a business</strong> (Track 12): many real micro-SaaS started exactly here — a mini-product that solved the builder's own problem, then others'. Your capstone might be your first income (12.1 will ask you to look at it that way).</li><li><strong>Confidence made real:</strong> after shipping one complete thing, «can I build AI products?» stops being a question. That certainty — earned, not claimed — is what carries you into the money tracks.</li></ul>"}
  ],
  worked: { t: "Worked example — a complete mini-product, every skill visible",
    html: "<div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>The idea (small, real):</em> «Receipt Wrangler» — a web page where a small-business owner drops receipt photos and gets back a clean expense spreadsheet, categorised, with a running total. Solves a real monthly pain; finishable in a focused day. <em>PLAN:</em> user = the owner; job = photos → categorised expense sheet; architecture = vision extraction (9.3) + structured outputs (9.5) + code for the totals (4.4) + a simple drag-drop web front-end; verification = reconciliation + UNREADABLE flagging; cost = Haiku (volume extraction) + caching the extraction prompt.</p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>BUILD (Claude Code, reviewed steps):</em> the API layer (vision + structured output schema + null-honesty prompt), the code layer (categorise, sum in code not model — 4.4), the interface (a clean single-page uploader — Ember-warm, obviously), the guardrails (spending limit, per-upload cap). <em>VERIFY:</em> real receipts including a blurry one (correctly flagged UNREADABLE, not guessed), totals reconcile, works on a phone, costs pennies per batch.</p></div><div class='wstep'><p class='nar'><em>SHIP:</em> README a stranger could follow, key in .env/.gitignored, pushed to GitHub, front-end deployed free. <em>Then he USES it</em> — his actual monthly receipt chore, done in two minutes. <em>Look at what one small product demonstrates:</em> prompt craft, vision, structured outputs, code-for-math, verification, cost engineering, an interface, shippability — the ENTIRE course, integrated, in a thing that works and that he can show any client or employer and say «I built this, here's the repo, try it.» That sentence is worth more than a shelf of certificates — and it's now true about YOU by the end of this lab.</em></p></div>"},
  lab: { where: "Claude Code — the capstone build",
    html: "<p>Build and ship your complete mini-product. Pick something small, real, and yours. Then run the full loop with Claude Code:</p>" + PB("prompt — the ship-it build", "I'm building a complete mini-product as my API capstone: [YOUR IDEA — what it does, for whom, its one job]. Let's do the full professional loop.\nPLAN first: propose the architecture (which model(s), tool use or vision, structured outputs, the interface), the verification plan, and a rough cost model — I'll review before we build.\nBUILD in reviewed small steps on a branch: the API integration (with proper prompts, structured outputs where my code parses results, and truth rules), the code layer, a simple usable interface (a clean web page is fine), and cost guardrails (spending limit, sane model choice, caching if there's a stable prefix).\nVERIFY: run it on real messy input including an edge case; confirm honest failure (no fabrication) and sane cost.\nSHIP: README a stranger could run, secrets in .env/.gitignored, ready to push. Tell me exactly how to deploy the front-end for free if I want it live.", "prompt") + "<p>Then USE it for real. Then write its one-paragraph portfolio description (13.3's format).</p>",
    expect: "A complete, working, shippable AI mini-product — every skill in this course integrated into one thing that solves a real problem, that you can demonstrate and hand over. This is the proof. Everything after this is about getting paid for what you can now provably do." },
  mistakes: [
    "<b>Building a demo instead of a product.</b> «Shows off the API» ≠ «solves a real job.» Pick a genuine problem — the realness is what makes it a portfolio piece and a possible business.",
    "<b>Skipping the interface.</b> A script only you can run isn't a product. The AI is the engine; a usable interface is what makes it a THING someone else can use (and you can show).",
    "<b>No verification or cost-awareness.</b> A product that fabricates on bad input or loses money per use isn't shippable — the reliability and economics layers aren't optional polish, they're what «complete» means.",
    "<b>Scope too big to finish.</b> A finished small product beats an abandoned ambitious one, always. Ruthlessly cut to one job you can ship in a focused day or two.",
    "<b>Not shipping it (README, repo, use).</b> An unfinished, undocumented build isn't proof of anything. Ship-standard (7.8) is what makes it count — for you and for the people you'll show."
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: "What makes a build a «complete mini-product» rather than a demo?",
      opts: ["It uses the newest model", "It solves a real job for a real user, with sound prompts, the right API features, reliability, cost-awareness, an interface, and shippability — every layer present", "It has the most code", "It's impressive to programmers"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Model choice is one small part.", "Correct — completeness is every LAYER present, integrated, working on real input. That integration is the actual skill employers and clients test.", "Less code that ships beats more code that doesn't.", "It should impress USERS by solving their problem — programmer-impressiveness is beside the point."] },
    { q: "Why is a shipped mini-product worth more than a certificate for getting hired or paid?",
      opts: ["Certificates are fake", "It's undeniable proof — a working, runnable thing you built demonstrates integrated skill directly, versus a certificate that only claims it", "It's longer", "Employers hate certificates"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Certificates have value (13.2), but they CLAIM skill.", "Correct — «here's the repo, try it» is proof; «I have a certificate» is a claim. In the rooms where money changes hands, proof wins (13.3/13.5).", "Length is irrelevant.", "They value both — but proof-of-work is the stronger signal, especially for building roles."] },
    { q: "The recommended way to build the capstone is:",
      opts: ["Write all the code by hand to prove you can", "Use the full 7.8 loop with Claude Code — plan (with verification and cost model), build in reviewed steps, verify on real input, ship to stranger-standard", "Skip planning and improvise", "Copy someone else's project"],
      a: 1,
      why: ["Commissioning-and-directing IS the 2026 skill (7.1) — hand-coding to 'prove' something misunderstands the job.", "Correct — the professional loop applied to a product; you direct and verify, the agent writes. This is exactly how real AI products get built now.", "Planning (architecture, verification, cost) before building is what separates a product from a mess.", "The point is to build YOUR thing that solves YOUR real problem — that's what makes it proof and possibly a business."] }
  ],
  prac: [
    { tag: "warm", q: "Brainstorm 5 mini-product ideas from YOUR real problems — each solvable with the API in a day, each with a clear user and one job. Then pick the one you'd actually use.",
      sol: "<b>Strong ideas share:</b> a real pain you have (so you're the first user and honest tester), one clear job (finishable), and a natural interface (upload/type in, get out). Examples: meeting-notes → action emails, receipts → expense sheet, a document Q&A over YOUR manuals, a personalised daily brief, bulk-rename/organise with a web front-end. The one you'd USE is the one to build — real usage is the ultimate verification." },
    { tag: "transfer", q: "Build and ship the capstone. Complete the full loop, use it for real, and write its portfolio paragraph (what it does, what it demonstrates, the repo link, how to try it).",
      sol: "<b>Portfolio paragraph shape:</b> «[Name]: [one-line what+who]. Built with [Claude API — vision/tools/structured outputs as relevant], featuring [verification + cost engineering]. It [the real result it produces]. Repo: [link] · Try it: [link]. Demonstrates: prompt engineering, API integration, structured/reliable output, and cost-aware architecture.» This paragraph is Track 13's portfolio, entry one — and the thing you'll link when a client or employer asks «what have you built?»" },
    { tag: "stretch", q: "Look at your shipped capstone through Track 12's lens: could it be a business? What would you need to add, who would pay, and what's the smallest version you could charge for?",
      sol: "<b>The evaluation:</b> (1) Who else has this exact pain? (if it's just you, it's a portfolio piece; if many small businesses do, it's a product). (2) What would 'sellable' require beyond 'works for me'? — usually: user accounts, data persistence, reliability at others' scale, support (the 4.5/8.4 'productisation is trust-engineering' gap). (3) Smallest chargeable version: could you offer it as a done-for-you SERVICE first (you run it for clients — Track 11) before building the full self-serve SaaS (Track 12)? — services validate demand at zero build-risk. <b>The insight:</b> your capstone sits exactly at the operator/builder boundary this whole course was built around — you can MONETISE it as a service tomorrow (deliver the outcome by hand-running your tool) or BUILD it into a product over months. That optionality — earned by being able to both operate and build — is precisely the position the money tracks exploit. You've reached the top of the builder phase. Everything you can now provably DO, Tracks 11-13 turn into what you GET PAID. The capstone isn't the end of learning; it's the beginning of earning.</p>" }
  ],
  recap: "Ship one complete mini-product — real job, sound prompts, the right API features, reliability, cost-awareness, an interface, stranger-runnable — integrating the whole course into undeniable proof you can build; it's your portfolio's first entry and possibly your first business.",
  review: "From Lesson 7.8: the plan-build-verify-commit loop was for code projects — here it ships a PRODUCT, adding the layers (interface, economics, real users) that turn a build into a business. The loop scaled up one final time.",
  bridge: "Phase D complete: you build, secure, and ship real AI products. Track 10 goes to the frontier — agents: the autonomous systems that run tool loops toward goals, the most powerful and most demanding thing you can build. Then Phase E collects: turning everything you can now do into money.",
  deep: "<p><strong>The full-stack AI builder — a genuinely rare and valuable position.</strong> Consider what shipping this capstone proves you can do, end to end: understand how the model works (Track 1), engineer its behaviour with prompts (Tracks 2–3), operate every surface expertly (Tracks 4–6), build and secure systems (Tracks 7–8), and integrate the API into a reliable, economical, usable product (Track 9). That FULL span — from mental model to shipped product, with security and economics — is held by remarkably few people in 2026. Most «AI users» stop at the app; most «prompt engineers» never ship products; most software engineers underrate the operator craft and the prompt discipline; most «AI enthusiasts» can't do the unglamorous economics and verification that make products real. You now sit at an intersection that the market prices highly precisely because it's narrow: the person who can take an AI idea from «what if» to «here's the running, profitable, secure thing» is exactly who companies need and can't easily hire, and who clients pay premium rates. The capstone is the artifact that PROVES you're that person — not claimed, demonstrated. And here's the deeper frame for what comes next: the capstone is where the two halves of this course fuse. The OPERATOR half (using AI to do work faster) and the BUILDER half (making AI systems for others) were taught separately, but they're one capability with two expressions — and the money tracks exploit exactly that duality: you can earn as an operator (services, done-for-you) starting tomorrow, and as a builder (products, systems) as you scale, using the SAME underlying skill. Most people are forced to choose one lane because they only have one skill; you have both, which means you have optionality most competitors don't. The final track of Phase D — agents — extends the builder half to its frontier. Then Phase E stops teaching you to DO things and starts teaching you to GET PAID for the formidable stack of things you can now, provably, do. You built the engine across nine tracks. Time to learn what it's worth.</p>"
};

})();
